window.P107_CONTENT = {"generated":"2026-10-15","modules":[{"num":1,"id":"regulations","file":"study-regulations.html","cat":"regulations","title":"Regulations","weight":"15 to 25%","minutes":25,"intro":"Part 107 is short: a few dozen sections of 14 CFR. The test asks about the numbers in it and about how the rules apply to realistic situations. This module explains the rules; the cheat sheet lists them section by section.","sections":[{"id":"numbers","title":"The numbers"},{"id":"who","title":"Who may act as remote PIC"},{"id":"limits","title":"Operating limitations"},{"id":"where","title":"Where you may fly"},{"id":"how","title":"How you must fly"},{"id":"people","title":"Operations over people"},{"id":"night","title":"Night operations"},{"id":"registration","title":"Registration and Remote ID"},{"id":"accidents","title":"Accidents, emergencies, inspections"},{"id":"waivers","title":"Waivers"},{"id":"tips","title":"Test tips"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"numbers\">The numbers the test wants you to know</h2>\n          <ul class=\"facts\">\n            <li><b>400 ft AGL</b><span>maximum altitude, or 400 ft above a structure within a 400 ft radius of it</span></li>\n            <li><b>87 kt</b><span>maximum groundspeed (100 mph)</span></li>\n            <li><b>3 sm</b><span>minimum visibility from the control station</span></li>\n            <li><b>500 / 2,000</b><span>feet below and horizontally from clouds</span></li>\n            <li><b>55 lb</b><span>aircraft must weigh less than this at takeoff, everything included</span></li>\n            <li><b>16</b><span>minimum age for the certificate</span></li>\n            <li><b>8 hr / 0.04</b><span>no alcohol within 8 hours; blood alcohol below 0.04 percent</span></li>\n            <li><b>10 days</b><span>to report a qualifying accident to the FAA</span></li>\n            <li><b>$500</b><span>property damage threshold for reporting (not counting the drone)</span></li>\n            <li><b>14 days</b><span>wait before retaking a failed knowledge test</span></li>\n            <li><b>24 months</b><span>recurrent training interval, in calendar months</span></li>\n            <li><b>30 days</b><span>to notify the FAA of an address change</span></li>\n            <li><b>120 days</b><span>validity of a temporary certificate</span></li>\n            <li><b>3 sm</b><span>visibility distance required of anti-collision lights at night</span></li>\n            <li><b>$5 / 3 yr</b><span>registration cost and validity per aircraft</span></li>\n            <li><b>1</b><span>aircraft per remote PIC at a time, without a waiver</span></li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"who\">Who may act as remote pilot in command</h2>\n          <p>Every Part 107 flight has a <strong>remote pilot in command (remote PIC)</strong>, designated before or during the flight, who is directly responsible for and the final authority over the operation (107.19). Someone else may manipulate the controls only under the remote PIC's direct supervision, where the remote PIC can take over immediately.</p>\n          <p>To act as remote PIC you must hold a remote pilot certificate with a small UAS rating (107.12) and have either passed the initial knowledge test or completed the FAA's recurrent training within the <strong>previous 24 calendar months</strong> (107.65). Calendar months means the requirement runs to the end of the month, two years later.</p>\n          <p>Eligibility (107.61): at least 16 years old; able to read, speak, write, and understand English (with limited medical exceptions); no physical or mental condition that would interfere with safe operation; and a passing knowledge test score, or for current Part 61 pilots, the FAA's online initial course.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout callout-reg\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Medical condition (107.17)</span>\n            <p>No one may act as remote PIC, visual observer, or control operator if they know, or have reason to know, of any physical or mental condition that would interfere with safe operation. That includes a bad cold, a new sedating medication, or a sleepless night. There is no medical certificate for Part 107; you self-certify every flight.</p>\n          </div>\n          <p><strong>Alcohol and drugs (107.27).</strong> The rules of 91.17 and 91.19 apply: no acting as a crewmember within 8 hours of consuming alcohol, while under its influence, while using any drug that affects your faculties contrary to safety, or with a blood alcohol concentration of 0.04 percent or more. A drug or alcohol conviction, or refusing a test, can cost you the certificate (107.57, 107.59).</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"limits\">Operating limitations (107.51)</h2>\n          <p>Four limits, all measured in a specific way:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Altitude:</strong> no higher than 400 ft above ground level, unless the aircraft is within a 400 ft radius of a structure and no more than 400 ft above the structure's uppermost limit. Inspecting a 600 ft tower, you may fly to 1,000 ft AGL while within 400 ft of it.</li>\n            <li><strong>Speed:</strong> groundspeed no greater than 87 knots (100 mph).</li>\n            <li><strong>Visibility:</strong> at least 3 statute miles, as observed from the location of the control station.</li>\n            <li><strong>Cloud clearance:</strong> at least 500 ft below and 2,000 ft horizontally from any cloud.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"callout callout-tip\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Test tip</span>\n            <p>Questions love the structure exception. Read for two conditions: within 400 ft of the structure <em>and</em> no more than 400 ft above its top. Both must be true. And note that AGL means above the ground directly below the aircraft, not above the launch point.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h2 id=\"where\">Where you may fly</h2>\n          <p><strong>Airspace authorization (107.41).</strong> You need prior ATC authorization to operate in Class B, C, or D airspace, or within the lateral boundaries of a Class E surface area designated for an airport. Class G needs no authorization. Class E that begins at 700 or 1,200 ft AGL does not reach you at 400 ft. Authorizations come through LAANC (automated, minutes) or FAADroneZone (manual, weeks). The <a href=\"#/study/airspace\">airspace module</a> covers how to tell which airspace you are in.</p>\n          <p><strong>Near airports (107.43).</strong> Even where no authorization is needed, you may not interfere with operations and traffic patterns at any airport, heliport, or seaplane base.</p>\n          <p><strong>Prohibited and restricted areas (107.45).</strong> Permission from the using or controlling agency is required.</p>\n          <p><strong>Flight restrictions (107.47).</strong> You must comply with TFRs and the special security instructions issued under 99.7, such as the standing restriction over stadiums during major sporting events (3 nautical miles, 3,000 ft AGL, one hour before to one hour after). TFRs are published as NOTAMs and are never printed on charts.</p>\n          <p><strong>State and local rules.</strong> The FAA regulates airspace, but states, counties, cities, and park agencies can regulate where you take off and land, trespass, privacy, and police powers. The National Park Service prohibits launching or landing inside national parks. Check every launch site.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"how\">How you must fly</h2>\n          <p><strong>Visual line of sight (107.31).</strong> Throughout the flight, the remote PIC, the person on the controls, or a visual observer must be able to see the aircraft with vision unaided by any device other than corrective lenses, well enough to know its location, attitude, altitude, and direction and to see and avoid other traffic. First-person-view goggles do not count. Binoculars may be used only momentarily.</p>\n          <p><strong>Visual observer (107.33).</strong> Optional, but if used, the remote PIC and VO must maintain effective communication at all times (a radio counts), and the VO must be able to see the aircraft unaided. A VO extends your scanning, not your range.</p>\n          <p><strong>One aircraft at a time (107.35).</strong> A person may not act as remote PIC, VO, or control operator for more than one unmanned aircraft at a time, without a waiver.</p>\n          <p><strong>Moving vehicles (107.25).</strong> You may not operate from a moving aircraft. You may operate from a moving land or water vehicle only over a sparsely populated area and not while carrying someone else's property for compensation.</p>\n          <p><strong>Right of way (107.37).</strong> Yield to all aircraft, airborne vehicles, and launch and reentry vehicles. Yielding means giving way and not passing over, under, or ahead of them unless well clear. A manned aircraft always wins. Also, no operating so close to another aircraft as to create a collision hazard.</p>\n          <p><strong>Careless or reckless operation and dropped objects (107.23).</strong> You may not operate in a careless or reckless manner that endangers life or property, and you may not allow an object to be dropped in a way that creates an undue hazard.</p>\n          <p><strong>Hazardous materials (107.36).</strong> Prohibited, and not waivable.</p>\n          <p><strong>Transporting property.</strong> Allowed for compensation within a state if everything else in Part 107 is met: under 55 lb total, VLOS, not from a moving vehicle. Carriage across state lines for hire falls under other economic regulations, which is why the FAA limits it to intrastate.</p>\n          <p><strong>Safe condition and preflight (107.15, 107.49).</strong> The aircraft must be in a condition for safe operation; discontinue the flight if continuing would be hazardous. Before every flight the remote PIC must assess the weather, airspace, and ground hazards, brief every participant, verify the control link, confirm there is enough power, and secure any payload.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"people\">Operations over people (107.39 and Subpart D)</h2>\n          <p>By default you may not fly over a human being unless that person is directly participating in the operation, or is under a covered structure or inside a stationary vehicle that can protect them from a falling aircraft. Since April 2021 there are four aircraft categories that allow flight over people without a waiver:</p>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Aircraft requirement</th><th>Over open-air assemblies?</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>1</td><td>0.55 lb or less including everything attached; no exposed rotating parts that could lacerate skin; no safety defects</td><td>Yes, sustained flight allowed only with Remote ID</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>2</td><td>FAA-accepted declaration of compliance: injury severity at or below 11 ft-lb of kinetic energy on impact, no exposed lacerating parts, no safety defects</td><td>Yes, with Remote ID</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>3</td><td>Declaration of compliance at or below 25 ft-lb; same other conditions</td><td>No. Over people only at a closed or restricted-access site where everyone is on notice, or with no sustained flight over any person not under cover</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>4</td><td>Airworthiness certificate under Part 21, maintained per its approved program</td><td>Yes, with Remote ID</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <p>Operations over moving vehicles (107.145) follow the same pattern: Categories 1 through 3 only within a closed or restricted-access site where people are on notice, or with no sustained flight over the vehicle; Category 4 under its operating limitations.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"night\">Night and civil twilight (107.29)</h2>\n          <p>Night operations are allowed without a waiver if two things are true: the remote PIC has completed the current initial test or recurrent training (which includes night knowledge), and the aircraft has <strong>anti-collision lighting visible for at least 3 statute miles</strong> with a flash rate sufficient to avoid a collision. The same lighting is required during civil twilight, defined in the contiguous United States as 30 minutes before official sunrise to 30 minutes after official sunset. The remote PIC may reduce the light's intensity only if it is in the interest of safety.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"registration\">Registration (Part 48) and Remote ID (Part 89)</h2>\n          <p>Every aircraft flown under Part 107 must be registered, regardless of weight (the 250 gram exemption is only for purely recreational flying). Register at FAADroneZone: $5 per aircraft, valid 3 years, and mark the registration number legibly on an external surface. Carry proof of registration.</p>\n          <p>Registered aircraft must broadcast Remote ID. Three ways to comply: <strong>standard Remote ID</strong> built into the aircraft (broadcasts aircraft and control station location); a <strong>broadcast module</strong> attached to the aircraft, listed on the registration, with the aircraft kept within VLOS of the operator (broadcasts the takeoff location instead); or operating only within an <strong>FAA-Recognized Identification Area (FRIA)</strong>. The requirement took effect September 16, 2023; the FAA's enforcement discretion ended March 16, 2024.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"accidents\">Accidents, emergencies, and inspections</h2>\n          <p><strong>Accident reporting (107.9).</strong> Within 10 calendar days, report to the FAA (through FAADroneZone or a FSDO) any operation that results in serious injury to a person or loss of consciousness, or damage to property other than the aircraft of $500 or more (repair cost or fair market value, whichever is lower). Damage to your own drone never counts.</p>\n          <p><strong>In-flight emergencies (107.21).</strong> The remote PIC may deviate from any rule of Part 107 to the extent necessary to meet the emergency, and must file a written report if the FAA asks.</p>\n          <p><strong>Inspection (107.7).</strong> You must present your certificate, the aircraft registration, and any other required document, and make the aircraft and records available for inspection or testing, upon request from the FAA, NTSB, TSA, or any federal, state, or local law enforcement officer.</p>\n          <p><strong>Falsification (107.5).</strong> False statements on any application, report, or record are grounds for denial, suspension, or revocation.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"waivers\">Waivers (Subpart E)</h2>\n          <p>The FAA can waive specific rules if you show the operation can be conducted safely under the terms of a certificate of waiver (107.200). Apply through FAADroneZone. Waivable rules (107.205) include operation from a moving vehicle, parts of the night rule, VLOS, visual observer, multiple aircraft, yielding right of way, operations over people, operations in certain airspace, and the operating limitations in 107.51. Not waivable: alcohol and drugs, careless operation, hazardous materials, and the certification requirements.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"tips\">Test tips for regulations questions</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Many questions are scenarios. Find the rule being tested, then check each answer against the exact wording. \"Sparsely populated\" is not \"rural\"; \"serious injury\" is not \"any injury\".</li>\n            <li>Watch units: feet AGL vs MSL, statute miles vs nautical miles, knots vs mph, calendar days vs days.</li>\n            <li>If an answer says a rule can never be waived, check the 107.205 list in your head.</li>\n            <li>The FAA's own sample questions (free, on the resources page) show the exact style.</li>\n          </ul>"},{"num":2,"id":"airspace","file":"study-airspace.html","cat":"airspace","title":"Airspace and chart reading","weight":"15 to 25%","minutes":35,"intro":"You will fly at 400 ft or lower, but the test expects you to read the same VFR sectional charts manned pilots use, because that is where controlled airspace, airports, obstacles, and special use areas are drawn. This is the module to spend extra time on.","sections":[{"id":"classes","title":"Airspace classes"},{"id":"chart","title":"The practice chart"},{"id":"airport-data","title":"Airport data blocks"},{"id":"obstacles","title":"Obstacles, terrain, MEF"},{"id":"sua","title":"Special use airspace"},{"id":"tfr","title":"TFRs and NOTAMs"},{"id":"laanc","title":"LAANC and facility maps"},{"id":"other","title":"Other chart features"},{"id":"tips","title":"Test tips"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"classes\">Airspace classes and what they mean for you</h2>\n          <p>Controlled airspace (Classes A through E) is where ATC provides services; uncontrolled airspace (Class G) is everything else. For a Part 107 pilot the only question that matters is whether 107.41 requires authorization: it does for Class B, C, D, and Class E surface areas, and it does not for Class G or for Class E that begins above the ground.</p>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Where</th><th>On the chart</th><th>Typical vertical limits</th><th>Part 107</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>A</td><td>Everywhere above 18,000 ft MSL</td><td>Not depicted</td><td>18,000 ft MSL to FL600</td><td>Not reachable</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>B</td><td>The busiest airports (Chicago O'Hare, Atlanta, LAX)</td><td>Solid blue lines, layered like an upside-down wedding cake</td><td>Surface to about 10,000 ft MSL; outer shelves have higher floors</td><td>Authorization required</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>C</td><td>Busy airports with radar approach control</td><td>Solid magenta lines; 5 nm core, 10 nm shelf</td><td>Surface to about 4,000 ft above the airport</td><td>Authorization required</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>D</td><td>Airports with a control tower</td><td>Dashed blue line, ceiling in brackets such as [-31]</td><td>Surface to about 2,500 ft above the airport</td><td>Authorization required</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>E surface area</td><td>Some airports with instrument approaches</td><td>Dashed magenta line</td><td>From the surface</td><td>Authorization required</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>E at 700 ft</td><td>Around most airports with instrument approaches</td><td>Magenta vignette (shaded band), 700 ft floor on the soft side</td><td>700 ft AGL up to 18,000 ft</td><td>No authorization below 700 ft</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>E at 1,200 ft</td><td>Most of the rest of the country</td><td>No marking, or a blue vignette where the floor changes</td><td>1,200 ft AGL up to 18,000 ft</td><td>No authorization below 1,200 ft</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>G</td><td>Below the Class E floor, away from busy airports</td><td>Not depicted; it is the absence of other markings</td><td>Surface up to the Class E floor</td><td>No authorization, but 107.43 still applies near airports</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Key fact</span>\n            <p>Blue is for towered airports and the airspace that needs a tower (B, D). Magenta is for everything else (C is the exception: magenta but towered, because it is defined by radar service). Dashed lines start at the surface. Vignettes start in the air.</p>\n          </div>\n          <p><strong>When a part-time tower closes</strong> (a star after the CT frequency), the Class D typically becomes Class E surface area or Class G. The Chart Supplement says which. Authorization requirements follow whatever airspace is active at the time of your flight.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"chart\">The practice chart</h2>\n          <p>This excerpt is fictional, but every symbol on it is drawn the way the real chart draws it. Tap to enlarge. Walk through it with the notes below, then expect to see it again in the practice exam.</p>\n          <figure>\n            <img class=\"zoomable\" src=\"img/practice-chart.svg\" alt=\"Practice sectional chart excerpt showing Riverton Regional airport inside Class D airspace, Millbrook airport inside a Class E surface area, restricted area R-4071, the Badger MOA, a VOR, a Victor airway, obstacles, a private airport, a heliport, a parachute area, and maximum elevation figures\" width=\"900\" height=\"620\" loading=\"lazy\">\n            <figcaption>Fictional practice chart, not for navigation. Tap to enlarge.</figcaption>\n          </figure>\n          <ol>\n            <li><strong>Riverton Regional (RVN)</strong> is drawn in blue with a runway layout, so it has a control tower and hard-surface runways. The dashed blue circle is Class D, and [-31] says the Class D ceiling is 3,100 ft MSL. Flying anywhere inside that circle, at any altitude, needs ATC authorization.</li>\n            <li>The <strong>magenta vignette</strong> around Riverton marks Class E with a 700 ft AGL floor on the shaded (inner) side. Below 700 ft you are in Class G, so a 400 ft flight at the farm southeast of the Class D ring needs no authorization.</li>\n            <li><strong>Millbrook (3K7)</strong> is magenta: no tower. The dashed magenta circle is a Class E surface area, which does require authorization.</li>\n            <li><strong>R-4071</strong> has a blue hatched border: a restricted area. <strong>Badger MOA</strong> has a magenta hatched border: a military operations area.</li>\n            <li>The <strong>obstacle</strong> southeast of the 88°30' line is 1,549 ft MSL and 1,049 ft AGL, so the ground there is about 500 ft MSL. The <strong>lighted obstacle group</strong> in the northeast has rays around the symbol and tops out at 2,349 ft MSL.</li>\n            <li>The large numbers with a small superscript are <strong>maximum elevation figures</strong>: the 2 with a small 5 means 2,500 ft MSL for that quadrangle.</li>\n            <li>The compass rose in the lower left is a <strong>VOR</strong> (Riverton, 114.2, RVN) and the light blue line leaving it is <strong>Victor airway V12</strong>, a Class E airway with a floor of 1,200 ft AGL.</li>\n            <li>The circle with an R is a <strong>private airport</strong>; the circle with an H is a <strong>heliport</strong>; the small parachute marks a <strong>parachute jumping area</strong>.</li>\n            <li>The dashed magenta line in the lower left labeled 3°W is an <strong>isogonic line</strong>: magnetic variation here is 3 degrees west.</li>\n            <li>The heavy black lines are <strong>latitude 42°00'N</strong> and <strong>longitude 88°30'W</strong>, with a tick every minute.</li>\n          </ol>\n\n          <h2 id=\"airport-data\">Airport data blocks</h2>\n          <p>Next to every public airport is a block of text. Riverton's reads:</p>\n          <pre>RIVERTON RGNL (RVN)\nCT - 118.3 ★   ATIS 124.6\n612  *L 54  122.95 ©</pre>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Name and identifier.</strong> Three-letter identifiers belong to larger airports; smaller fields have alphanumeric ones like 3K7.</li>\n            <li><strong>CT - 118.3 ★.</strong> Control tower frequency. The star means the tower is part time.</li>\n            <li><strong>ATIS 124.6.</strong> The recorded airport information broadcast.</li>\n            <li><strong>612.</strong> Field elevation in feet MSL. Add 400 to get your maximum MSL altitude over the field.</li>\n            <li><strong>*L.</strong> Runway lighting exists; the star means there are limitations (pilot-controlled, part-time, or on request). See the Chart Supplement.</li>\n            <li><strong>54.</strong> Longest runway in hundreds of feet: 5,400 ft.</li>\n            <li><strong>122.95 ©.</strong> The circled C marks the common traffic advisory frequency (CTAF), where pilots self-announce. At a towered field the CTAF is usually the tower frequency; here UNICOM 122.95 is used when the tower is closed.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>Millbrook's block adds <strong>RP 18</strong>: right-hand traffic pattern for runway 18. Standard patterns use left turns, so RP tells you which side of the runway low-flying traffic will be on. A <strong>NO SVFR</strong> note means special VFR is not available; a <strong>(Pvt)</strong> label means private.</p>\n          <p>Airport symbols: a plain circle is a field with runways shorter than 1,500 ft or soft surfaces; a circle with a runway layout inside means hard-surfaced runways of 1,500 to 8,069 ft; runway layouts drawn without a circle mean at least one runway longer than 8,069 ft. Small tick marks around a circle mean fuel and services are available during normal working hours.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"obstacles\">Obstacles, terrain, and maximum elevation figures</h2>\n          <p>Obstacle symbols come with two numbers: the top in feet <strong>MSL</strong> in bold, and the height <strong>AGL</strong> in parentheses. Charts show obstacles 200 ft AGL and taller. Obstacles over 1,000 ft AGL use a taller symbol. A symbol with small rays is lighted. Two symbols close together with one set of numbers is a group obstacle. Remember that guyed towers have wires extending far out from the base, often invisible against the sky.</p>\n          <p><strong>Maximum elevation figures</strong> appear in every 30 minute quadrangle as a large number (thousands of feet) with a small superscript (hundreds). An MEF of 1<sup>7</sup> is 1,700 ft MSL: the highest terrain or obstacle in the box, rounded up with a margin. It tells you the general level of the land, which you need to convert a METAR cloud base or a chart altitude into something you can compare with 400 ft AGL.</p>\n          <p>Terrain is shown with contour lines and color tints that get browner with elevation. Spot elevations (a small dot with a number) mark the highest point in an area.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout callout-tip\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Test tip</span>\n            <p>A favorite question: \"What is the maximum altitude you may fly near obstacle X?\" Subtract the AGL number from the MSL number to get the ground elevation, add 400. If the question says you will be within 400 ft of the obstacle, add 400 to the obstacle's MSL top instead.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h2 id=\"sua\">Special use airspace</h2>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Label</th><th>Border</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Prohibited area</td><td>P-56</td><td>Blue hatched</td><td>Flight prohibited for national security. Permission required and rarely granted.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Restricted area</td><td>R-4071</td><td>Blue hatched</td><td>Invisible hazards such as gunnery or missiles. Permission from the controlling agency required when active. Hours and agency are in the chart margin tabulation and the Chart Supplement.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Warning area</td><td>W-237</td><td>Blue hatched</td><td>Same hazards as restricted, but over water from 3 nm offshore where the U.S. lacks sole jurisdiction.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Military operations area</td><td>BADGER MOA</td><td>Magenta hatched</td><td>Military training; separates military from IFR traffic. Not prohibited to you, but fast low traffic. Check activity and use extreme caution.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Alert area</td><td>A-211</td><td>Magenta hatched</td><td>High volume of pilot training or unusual activity. No permission needed; everyone is responsible for collision avoidance.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Controlled firing area</td><td>Not charted</td><td>Not charted</td><td>Activity stops when an aircraft approaches, so it is not depicted.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>National security area</td><td>NSA</td><td>Broken magenta line</td><td>Voluntary avoidance; can become mandatory by NOTAM.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Military training route</td><td>IR1234, VR1234</td><td>Gray lines with arrows</td><td>High-speed military traffic. Four-digit numbers: segments at or below 1,500 ft AGL. Three digits: some segments above 1,500 ft.</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n\n          <h2 id=\"tfr\">TFRs and NOTAMs</h2>\n          <p><strong>Temporary flight restrictions</strong> are issued by NOTAM for things that cannot be printed on a chart: presidential movements, wildfires, disasters, space launches, air shows, and sporting events. The stadium TFR is standing: 3 nautical miles and 3,000 ft AGL around stadiums seating 30,000 or more, from one hour before to one hour after MLB, NFL, NCAA Division I football, and major motor-sports events. Violating a TFR is a serious enforcement matter.</p>\n          <p>Check before every flight: <a href=\"https://tfr.faa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">tfr.faa.gov</a>, the FAA NOTAM search, a Flight Service briefing at 1-800-WX-BRIEF, or an FAA-approved airspace app. The chart tells you where the permanent airspace is; NOTAMs tell you what has changed today.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"laanc\">LAANC and UAS Facility Maps</h2>\n          <p><strong>LAANC</strong> (Low Altitude Authorization and Notification Capability) is how most Part 107 pilots get the authorization 107.41 requires. Through an FAA-approved service supplier's app you draw your operating area, enter a time window and altitude, and receive an automatic approval in near real time, provided the altitude is at or below the pre-approved ceiling in the <strong>UAS Facility Map</strong> grid for that spot.</p>\n          <p>Facility map grids show numbers such as 0, 50, 100, 200, 300, or 400: the maximum altitude ATC will auto-approve, in feet AGL. A 0 grid (typically on the extended runway centerlines) means no automatic approval; you may apply through FAADroneZone for further coordination, which is reviewed by hand. LAANC can also submit requests above the grid ceiling, up to 400 ft, for manual review. Authorizations are limited to the exact area, altitude, and time approved, and ATC can modify or cancel them at any time.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"other\">Other features worth knowing</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Latitude and longitude.</strong> Lines every 30 minutes, ticks every minute. Latitude is north-south (horizontal lines), longitude is east-west (vertical lines). In the continental U.S. longitudes are west, so numbers increase to the left.</li>\n            <li><strong>VORs and airways.</strong> A VOR is a ground navigation station drawn with a compass rose oriented to magnetic north. Victor airways (V-numbers, light blue) connect them at 1,200 ft AGL and above.</li>\n            <li><strong>Isogonic lines.</strong> Dashed magenta lines showing magnetic variation. Runway numbers, ATC headings, and VOR radials are magnetic; the chart grid is true. Winds in written reports are true; winds from a tower or ATIS are magnetic.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mode C veil.</strong> A thin blue ring 30 nm around Class B airports. Manned aircraft need transponders inside it. It does not by itself require anything from a Part 107 pilot.</li>\n            <li><strong>Parks and refuges.</strong> National parks, wildlife refuges, and wilderness areas are outlined with a blue line and dots. Pilots are requested to stay 2,000 ft above them; drone takeoff and landing on National Park Service land is prohibited by the Park Service.</li>\n            <li><strong>Chart currency.</strong> Sectionals are revised every 56 days. Digital charts are free from the FAA.</li>\n            <li><strong>Chart Supplement.</strong> The companion book (formerly the Airport/Facility Directory) with airport hours, frequencies, runway details, parachute activity, and special use airspace contacts.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"tips\">Test tips for airspace questions</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>The real test uses figures from the Airman Knowledge Testing Supplement. Download it (free, on the <a href=\"#/ref/resources\">resources page</a>) and practice finding airports by latitude and longitude, reading data blocks, and identifying airspace by line style and color.</li>\n            <li>Ask yourself three questions about any point on a chart: What is the lowest airspace here? Does it start at the surface? Do I need authorization?</li>\n            <li>Bracketed numbers are Class D ceilings in MSL. Fractions on Class B and C shelves are ceiling over floor in hundreds of feet MSL. SFC means surface.</li>\n            <li>Do not confuse a MOA (caution) with a restricted area (permission) with a prohibited area (no).</li>\n          </ul>"},{"num":3,"id":"weather","file":"study-weather.html","cat":"weather","title":"Weather","weight":"11 to 16%","minutes":30,"intro":"The weather questions are half decoding (reading a METAR or TAF) and half theory (what stable air does, what a cold front brings). Both halves reward memorizing a small set of patterns.","sections":[{"id":"minimums","title":"Part 107 minimums"},{"id":"metar","title":"Decoding a METAR"},{"id":"taf","title":"Decoding a TAF"},{"id":"products","title":"Other products"},{"id":"atmosphere","title":"The atmosphere"},{"id":"stability","title":"Stable vs unstable air"},{"id":"clouds","title":"Clouds and fog"},{"id":"fronts","title":"Fronts"},{"id":"storms","title":"Thunderstorms"},{"id":"wind","title":"Wind and wind shear"},{"id":"density","title":"Density altitude"},{"id":"tips","title":"Test tips"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"minimums\">The Part 107 weather minimums</h2>\n          <ul class=\"facts\">\n            <li><b>3 sm</b><span>minimum visibility from the control station</span></li>\n            <li><b>500 ft</b><span>below clouds</span></li>\n            <li><b>2,000 ft</b><span>horizontally from clouds</span></li>\n            <li><b>900 ft AGL</b><span>the lowest ceiling that lets you fly at 400 ft legally</span></li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>That last number is a derived one: 400 ft of altitude plus 500 ft of cloud clearance. A METAR reporting BKN008 (broken at 800 ft) grounds a 400 ft flight.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"metar\">Decoding a METAR</h2>\n          <p>A METAR is an hourly observation. Read this one left to right:</p>\n          <pre>KRVN 151953Z 27015G24KT 10SM SCT035 BKN050 24/09 A2998 RMK AO2</pre>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th class=\"mono\">Group</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">KRVN</td><td>Station identifier. U.S. airports start with K.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">151953Z</td><td>Day 15 of the month, 1953 UTC. Z means Zulu (UTC). Central Daylight Time is UTC minus 5, so this is 2:53 PM CDT.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">27015G24KT</td><td>Wind from 270° true at 15 knots, gusting to 24. VRB means variable direction (light winds). 00000KT is calm. A group like 240V300 after the wind shows the direction varying between 240° and 300°.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">10SM</td><td>Visibility 10 statute miles. Fractions are possible: 1 1/2SM. In TAFs, P6SM means more than 6 miles.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">SCT035 BKN050</td><td>Scattered clouds at 3,500 ft AGL, broken at 5,000 ft AGL. Heights are in hundreds of feet above ground. The broken layer is the ceiling.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">24/09</td><td>Temperature 24 °C, dew point 9 °C. M05 would mean minus 5.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">A2998</td><td>Altimeter setting 29.98 inches of mercury.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">RMK AO2</td><td>Remarks. AO2 means an automated station with a precipitation sensor.</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <p><strong>Sky cover:</strong> SKC or CLR (clear), FEW (1/8 to 2/8), SCT (3/8 to 4/8), BKN (5/8 to 7/8), OVC (8/8). A <strong>ceiling</strong> is the lowest broken or overcast layer, or the vertical visibility (VV) into fog or precipitation. A layer with CB (cumulonimbus) or TCU (towering cumulus) appended means thunderstorms.</p>\n          <p><strong>Weather codes</strong> go between visibility and clouds: RA rain, SN snow, DZ drizzle, TS thunderstorm, SH showers, FG fog (visibility below 5/8 mile), BR mist, HZ haze, FZ freezing, GR hail. A minus sign is light, no sign is moderate, a plus is heavy: -RA, RA, +RA. SPECI in place of METAR means a special, off-schedule observation because conditions changed significantly.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"taf\">Decoding a TAF</h2>\n          <p>A Terminal Aerodrome Forecast covers the area within 5 statute miles of an airport, usually for 24 or 30 hours, in METAR-style code with time groups:</p>\n          <pre>TAF KRVN 151720Z 1518/1618 27012KT P6SM SCT040\n     FM160200 30006KT P6SM SKC\n     TEMPO 1610/1613 4SM BR\n     FM161500 22010G18KT P6SM BKN030</pre>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>151720Z</strong> issued on the 15th at 1720Z; <strong>1518/1618</strong> valid from the 15th at 1800Z to the 16th at 1800Z.</li>\n            <li><strong>FM</strong> (from) begins a new period with a complete change: FM160200 means from the 16th at 0200Z.</li>\n            <li><strong>TEMPO</strong> describes temporary fluctuations lasting less than an hour at a time within the period shown: between 1000Z and 1300Z, expect brief periods of 4 miles in mist.</li>\n            <li><strong>BECMG</strong> (becoming) is a gradual change over the time range; <strong>PROB30</strong> is a 30 percent probability.</li>\n            <li><strong>P6SM</strong> is visibility greater than 6 statute miles; <strong>SKC</strong> is sky clear.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"products\">Other weather products</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>PIREPs (UA, or UUA for urgent)</strong> are reports from pilots in flight: the only source of actual turbulence, icing, and cloud-top information.</li>\n            <li><strong>SIGMETs</strong> warn of weather hazardous to all aircraft: severe turbulence, severe icing, dust or sand storms, volcanic ash. <strong>Convective SIGMETs</strong> cover thunderstorms. <strong>AIRMETs</strong> (now issued graphically as G-AIRMETs) cover moderate conditions: Sierra for IFR conditions and mountain obscuration, Tango for turbulence and surface winds over 30 knots, Zulu for icing.</li>\n            <li><strong>Winds and temperatures aloft (FB)</strong> forecast winds at 3,000 ft and above. Not directly useful at 400 ft, but they show whether winds strengthen with height.</li>\n            <li><strong>Graphical Forecasts for Aviation (GFA)</strong> on aviationweather.gov replaced the old text area forecasts and are the easiest way to see the big picture.</li>\n            <li><strong>Flight Service</strong> at 1-800-WX-BRIEF provides a standard briefing, including TFRs.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"atmosphere\">The atmosphere in six facts</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Standard sea-level conditions: <strong>15 °C (59 °F) and 29.92 inHg</strong> (1013.2 hPa).</li>\n            <li>Temperature falls about <strong>2 °C per 1,000 ft</strong> (the standard lapse rate); pressure falls about 1 inHg per 1,000 ft in the lower atmosphere.</li>\n            <li>Warm air is less dense than cold air; humid air is less dense than dry air; low pressure means less dense air. All three reduce aircraft performance.</li>\n            <li>The <strong>dew point</strong> is the temperature at which air becomes saturated. When temperature and dew point meet, you get clouds, fog, or dew. A spread of 2 °C or less with a falling temperature is a fog warning.</li>\n            <li>Most weather happens in the <strong>troposphere</strong>, which extends to roughly 36,000 ft at mid latitudes.</li>\n            <li>Uneven heating of the Earth's surface drives everything: pressure differences, wind, convection, and the water cycle.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"stability\">Stable versus unstable air</h2>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Stable air</th><th>Unstable air</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Resists vertical motion</td><td>Rises freely once lifted</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Stratiform (layered) clouds</td><td>Cumuliform (lumpy, towering) clouds</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Steady precipitation</td><td>Showery precipitation</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Smooth air</td><td>Turbulence</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Poor visibility (haze, smoke, fog trapped near the ground)</td><td>Good visibility (vertical mixing clears the air)</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Often under a temperature inversion</td><td>Often on hot afternoons or behind a cold front</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <p>A <strong>temperature inversion</strong>, where temperature increases with height, is the most stable condition of all. It traps haze and smoke, gives smooth air, and can hide a sharp wind shear between calm surface air and moving air above. Inversions form on clear, calm nights as the ground cools.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout callout-tip\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Test tip</span>\n            <p>If a question describes smooth air with poor visibility, the answer involves stable air or an inversion. Turbulence with good visibility and showers means unstable air. The test rarely asks anything subtler than this table.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h2 id=\"clouds\">Clouds and fog</h2>\n          <p>Clouds are grouped by height: <strong>low</strong> (stratus, stratocumulus, nimbostratus, up to about 6,500 ft), <strong>middle</strong> (altostratus, altocumulus, 6,500 to 20,000 ft), <strong>high</strong> (cirrus, cirrostratus, cirrocumulus, above 20,000 ft, made of ice), and <strong>vertical development</strong> (towering cumulus, cumulonimbus). For a drone pilot the important ones are the low clouds that set your ceiling and the vertically developed ones that mean turbulence, downdrafts, and lightning. <strong>Lenticular</strong> clouds near mountains mark mountain wave turbulence even though they look stationary.</p>\n          <p><strong>Fog</strong> is a cloud at the surface, and it is the weather most likely to catch you on a calm morning:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Radiation fog:</strong> clear, calm nights; the ground radiates heat away and the air above it cools to the dew point. Common in valleys and fields, burns off after sunrise.</li>\n            <li><strong>Advection fog:</strong> moist air moves (is advected) over a colder surface such as cold water or snow. Needs wind; common on coasts; can persist for days.</li>\n            <li><strong>Upslope fog:</strong> moist air cools as it is pushed up terrain.</li>\n            <li><strong>Steam fog:</strong> cold air over warm water, common over lakes in fall.</li>\n            <li><strong>Precipitation-induced (frontal) fog:</strong> warm rain falling through cool air ahead of a warm front.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"fronts\">Fronts</h2>\n          <p>A front is the boundary between two air masses. The test wants the contrast between the two common ones:</p>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Cold front</th><th>Warm front</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Cold air pushes under warm air; steep slope, moves fast</td><td>Warm air slides over cold air; shallow slope, moves slowly</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Narrow band of towering cumulus, thunderstorms, heavy showers</td><td>Wide area of stratus, drizzle, fog, low ceilings, poor visibility ahead of the front</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Abrupt wind shift, pressure rises after passage</td><td>Gradual changes</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Clearing, cooler, gusty air behind it</td><td>Warmer, humid air behind it</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <p>An <strong>occluded front</strong> forms when a fast cold front overtakes a warm front and can combine the worst of both. A <strong>stationary front</strong> stalls and can bring days of the same weather. Squall lines, which are bands of severe thunderstorms, often form well ahead of a fast-moving cold front.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"storms\">Thunderstorms</h2>\n          <p>Three ingredients: <strong>moisture, unstable air, and a lifting action</strong> (heating, terrain, or a front). Three stages:</p>\n          <ol>\n            <li><strong>Cumulus stage:</strong> updrafts build the cloud. Updrafts can exceed 3,000 ft per minute.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mature stage:</strong> precipitation reaches the surface, downdrafts begin alongside the updrafts. The most violent stage: heavy rain, hail, lightning, wind shear, microbursts, and gust fronts that race ahead of the storm.</li>\n            <li><strong>Dissipating stage:</strong> downdrafts dominate and the storm collapses.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <p>Hazards extend well beyond the cloud: lightning can strike 10 miles from rain, and the outflow gust front can knock a small aircraft down minutes before a storm arrives. Manned pilots are told to stay 20 miles from severe storms; a drone has no business being airborne when a thunderstorm is anywhere near.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"wind\">Wind and wind shear</h2>\n          <p>Wind flows from high to low pressure, deflected by the Coriolis force. Near the surface, friction slows it and bends it toward low pressure. Wind usually increases with altitude, even within your 400 ft: expect stronger wind at the top of a climb than at the launch point.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Sea breeze:</strong> during the day, land heats faster than water, air over land rises, and wind blows from the water onto the land. At night the <strong>land breeze</strong> reverses it.</li>\n            <li><strong>Mechanical turbulence:</strong> wind tumbling over buildings, trees, and terrain creates eddies that extend downwind. Launch upwind of obstacles, not in their wake.</li>\n            <li><strong>Convective turbulence:</strong> thermals rising from heated surfaces (parking lots, dark fields) on sunny afternoons.</li>\n            <li><strong>Wind shear:</strong> a sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance, horizontal or vertical. It can occur at any altitude and is most dangerous near thunderstorms, fronts, and temperature inversions, where a layer of still air sits under moving air. Low-level wind shear can overpower a small aircraft's ability to hold position.</li>\n            <li><strong>Microbursts:</strong> intense downdrafts from thunderstorms that spread out at the surface, producing shear in every direction.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"density\">Density altitude</h2>\n          <p><strong>Pressure altitude</strong> is the altitude in the standard atmosphere that matches the current pressure. <strong>Density altitude</strong> is pressure altitude corrected for temperature: the altitude at which the air density equals what you have now. Hot, high, humid, and low pressure all raise density altitude, and the aircraft performs as if it were at that higher altitude: less thrust, less lift, slower climb, longer takeoff roll for fixed wings, and shorter endurance for multirotors because the motors draw more current to hover.</p>\n          <p><strong>Temperature</strong> also hits the battery directly. Cold lithium batteries deliver less capacity and sag under load; warm them before flight and plan a larger reserve. Heat accelerates battery aging and can trigger thermal protection on controllers and cameras.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"tips\">Test tips for weather questions</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Practice decoding until every group in a METAR is automatic: time, wind, visibility, weather, clouds, temperature and dew point, altimeter. Expect at least one question that is pure decoding.</li>\n            <li>Convert Zulu time with your own offset (Central: UTC minus 5 in daylight time, minus 6 in standard time).</li>\n            <li>Learn the stable/unstable table and the cold/warm front table cold. Most theory questions come from them.</li>\n            <li>Density altitude is always about performance getting worse when it goes up.</li>\n          </ul>"},{"num":4,"id":"performance","file":"study-performance.html","cat":"loading","title":"Loading and performance","weight":"7 to 11%","minutes":15,"intro":"The smallest knowledge area on the test, but every question in it follows from a handful of physical ideas: where the weight sits, how much there is, how dense the air is, and how hard the aircraft is being asked to turn.","sections":[{"id":"forces","title":"The four forces"},{"id":"weight","title":"Weight and balance"},{"id":"loadfactor","title":"Load factor and turns"},{"id":"stalls","title":"Angle of attack and stalls"},{"id":"stability","title":"Stability"},{"id":"density","title":"Density altitude and performance"},{"id":"battery","title":"Batteries and endurance"},{"id":"data","title":"Determining performance"},{"id":"tips","title":"Test tips"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"forces\">The four forces</h2>\n          <p>Lift opposes weight; thrust opposes drag. In steady flight they balance. A fixed-wing aircraft gets lift from its wing; a multirotor gets it from its propellers, which is why a multirotor tilts to move: tilting the thrust vector produces a horizontal component.</p>\n          <p>Lift depends on air density, airspeed squared, wing (or rotor disk) area, and angle of attack. Anything that reduces density or demands more lift costs power.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"weight\">Weight and balance</h2>\n          <p>The <strong>center of gravity (CG)</strong> is the point where the aircraft would balance. Manufacturers define a range of acceptable CG positions and a maximum takeoff weight. Both are easy to violate with a drone: a bigger battery, a camera on a longer gimbal, a payload hung off one side, prop guards, a Remote ID module, or a light bar all add weight and may move the CG.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Overweight:</strong> more thrust needed, so higher current draw, shorter endurance, slower climb, less margin against wind and gusts, and more stress on motors and arms.</li>\n            <li><strong>CG off center on a multirotor:</strong> the flight controller levels the aircraft by running the motors on the heavy side harder. That works until it does not: endurance drops and a gust can exceed the remaining authority.</li>\n            <li><strong>Forward CG on a fixed wing:</strong> nose heavy, more stable, but the tail must push down harder, adding drag and raising stall speed.</li>\n            <li><strong>Aft CG on a fixed wing:</strong> less stable, more sensitive controls, harder stall recovery. Beyond the limit, possibly unrecoverable.</li>\n            <li><strong>Shifting payload:</strong> a package that slides in flight moves the CG in flight. 107.49 requires the remote PIC to verify that any object carried is secure and does not affect controllability.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>Compute takeoff weight by adding everything: airframe, battery, payload, and accessories. Weigh it on a kitchen scale rather than trusting spec sheets. The same total must stay under 55 lb to remain a small unmanned aircraft under Part 107, and under 0.55 lb to qualify for Category 1 operations over people.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"loadfactor\">Load factor and turns</h2>\n          <p><strong>Load factor</strong> is the ratio of the load the wings or rotors are supporting to the actual weight of the aircraft, expressed in Gs. In straight and level flight it is 1. In a level turn the lift must support the weight and also pull the aircraft around the turn, so load factor rises with bank angle:</p>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Bank angle</th><th>Load factor</th><th>Stall speed increase (fixed wing)</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>0°</td><td>1.0 G</td><td>none</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>30°</td><td>1.15 G</td><td>about 7 percent</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>45°</td><td>1.41 G</td><td>about 19 percent</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>60°</td><td>2.0 G</td><td>about 41 percent</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>75°</td><td>3.9 G</td><td>about 97 percent</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <p>Load factor equals 1 divided by the cosine of the bank angle. Stall speed rises with the square root of the load factor. Gusts and abrupt pull-ups also raise it. The FAA testing supplement includes a load factor chart; expect a question that asks you to read it.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"stalls\">Angle of attack and stalls</h2>\n          <p>The <strong>angle of attack</strong> is the angle between the wing's chord line and the relative wind. Lift increases with angle of attack until the <strong>critical angle of attack</strong>, where airflow separates from the upper surface and the wing stalls. A stall depends on angle of attack only: it can happen at any airspeed, in any attitude, at any power setting. Fast, steep, or heavy flight raises the speed at which the critical angle is reached.</p>\n          <p>Recovery: reduce the angle of attack (lower the nose), then add power to minimize altitude loss. Multirotors do not stall in the fixed-wing sense, but they have an equivalent: ask for more thrust than the motors can give (heavy, hot, high, or a steep descent into your own downwash) and the aircraft settles.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"stability\">Stability</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Positive static stability:</strong> after a disturbance the aircraft tends to return toward its original attitude.</li>\n            <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> it stays in the new attitude.</li>\n            <li><strong>Negative:</strong> it keeps diverging.</li>\n            <li><strong>Dynamic stability</strong> describes what happens over time: oscillations that damp out (positive), stay the same (neutral), or grow (negative).</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>Most multirotors are aerodynamically unstable and rely entirely on the flight controller. Lose the controller's sensors (a GPS loss drops most aircraft to attitude mode) and the aircraft stays level but drifts with the wind.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"density\">Density altitude and performance</h2>\n          <p>High temperature, high elevation, high humidity, and low pressure all make the air less dense. Propellers produce less thrust, wings produce less lift, and combustion engines produce less power. For a multirotor the practical effects are a lower maximum takeoff weight, slower climbs, hotter motors, and shorter flights. On a summer afternoon at a mountain site, a quadcopter rated for 30 minutes at sea level may do 20.</p>\n          <p>Wind adds to the load: holding position in a 20 knot breeze is flying at 20 knots. Battery drain climbs steeply with wind speed, and a return flight into the wind takes longer than the trip out.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"battery\">Batteries and endurance</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Lithium polymer and lithium-ion packs lose usable capacity when cold, age faster when hot, and are damaged by over-discharge. Land with a reserve (20 to 25 percent is a common rule), and treat the manufacturer's warning levels as limits, not targets.</li>\n            <li>A pack's voltage sags under heavy load. Aggressive flying, heavy payloads, and cold all deepen the sag, which is why a battery can show plenty of charge and still trigger a low-voltage landing.</li>\n            <li>Endurance figures from the manufacturer assume a light aircraft, still air, and a new battery. Log your own flights and use your numbers.</li>\n            <li>Inspect packs before every flight: swelling, dents, damaged connectors, or heat after charging mean the pack is retired.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"data\">Determining performance</h2>\n          <p>107.73 lists \"determining the performance of small unmanned aircraft\" as a knowledge area. In practice it means: start with the manufacturer's documentation (maximum takeoff weight, wind limits, temperature range, endurance), understand how your conditions differ from the ones those numbers assume, and verify with test flights and a log. A preflight performance check is part of the 107.49 assessment: do you have enough power for the planned operation with a reserve, given this weight, this wind, and this temperature?</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"tips\">Test tips for loading and performance questions</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Weight goes up: performance goes down. Density altitude goes up: performance goes down. Those two sentences answer most questions.</li>\n            <li>Know 2 G at 60° of bank and roughly 1.4 G at 45°.</li>\n            <li>A stall is about angle of attack, not speed. Any answer that says \"a stall only happens at low speed\" is wrong.</li>\n            <li>Forward CG: stable but less efficient. Aft CG: unstable.</li>\n          </ul>"},{"num":5,"id":"operations","file":"study-operations.html","cat":"operations","title":"Operations","weight":"35 to 45%","minutes":35,"intro":"The biggest slice of the test, covering eight of the knowledge areas in 107.73: preflight and maintenance, crew resource management, decision making, emergencies, radio procedures, airport operations, night operations, and the physiological effects of drugs, alcohol, fatigue, and stress. Most of it is common sense with vocabulary attached.","sections":[{"id":"preflight","title":"Preflight"},{"id":"crm","title":"Crew resource management"},{"id":"adm","title":"Decision making"},{"id":"risk","title":"Risk management"},{"id":"emergencies","title":"Emergencies"},{"id":"radio","title":"Radio communication"},{"id":"airports","title":"Airport operations"},{"id":"night","title":"Night operations"},{"id":"physiology","title":"Physiology"},{"id":"maintenance","title":"Maintenance"},{"id":"tips","title":"Test tips"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"preflight\">Preflight (107.49)</h2>\n          <p>Before every flight the remote PIC must do three things. First, <strong>assess the operating environment</strong>: local weather, local airspace and any flight restrictions, the location of people and property on the surface, and other ground hazards such as wires, towers, and trees. Second, <strong>brief every participant</strong> on operating conditions, emergency procedures, contingency procedures, roles and responsibilities, and potential hazards. Third, <strong>inspect the aircraft</strong>: confirm it is in a condition for safe operation, that the control links between the control station and the aircraft work, that there is enough power for the intended operation, and that any object carried is secure and does not adversely affect flight characteristics or controllability.</p>\n          <p>A useful preflight follows the manufacturer's checklist and adds: airspace and TFR check, battery condition and charge, propeller condition and tightness, airframe cracks, gimbal and payload mounts, compass and GPS status, firmware verified, return-to-home point and altitude set, takeoff and landing area clear, and a plan for where the aircraft goes if the link drops. The <a href=\"#/ref/safety\">safety page</a> has a printable version.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"crm\">Crew resource management</h2>\n          <p><strong>CRM</strong> is the effective use of all available resources: people, equipment, and information. Airlines invented it after accidents in which crews failed to speak up or the captain failed to listen. For a drone crew it means:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Clear roles: who is remote PIC, who is on the controls, who is the visual observer, who manages the client and the bystanders.</li>\n            <li>A briefing before flight and a debrief after.</li>\n            <li>Standard communication: clock positions for traffic (\"traffic, two o'clock, low\"), compass directions for the aircraft, call-outs for altitude and battery, and a word that means stop.</li>\n            <li>A <strong>sterile cockpit</strong>: no unnecessary conversation during takeoff, landing, and emergencies.</li>\n            <li>Assertiveness: anyone on the crew can and should call a hazard. The remote PIC decides, but should want to hear it.</li>\n            <li>Shared <strong>situational awareness</strong>: everyone knows where the aircraft is, what it is doing, and what traffic is around. Warning signs of lost awareness include fixation on the screen, confusion, ambiguity about the aircraft's position, and losing track of time or battery.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>A <strong>visual observer</strong> (107.33) extends the crew's eyes. The VO must be able to see the aircraft unaided and must be in effective communication with the remote PIC at all times. A VO does not fly, cannot cover a second aircraft, and does not let the aircraft go beyond the remote PIC's own ability to see it.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"adm\">Aeronautical decision making</h2>\n          <p><strong>ADM</strong> is a systematic approach to choosing the best course of action in a given set of circumstances. The FAA teaches several memory aids, and the test asks about all of them.</p>\n          <h3>PAVE: identifying risk before flight</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>P</strong>ilot: your health, fatigue, currency, experience with this aircraft and this kind of job.</li>\n            <li><strong>A</strong>ircraft: condition, battery, performance limits, payload.</li>\n            <li>en<strong>V</strong>ironment: weather, airspace, terrain, obstacles, people, time of day.</li>\n            <li><strong>E</strong>xternal pressures: the client waiting, the money, the schedule, the urge to get the shot.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>IMSAFE: am I fit to fly?</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>I</strong>llness, <strong>M</strong>edication, <strong>S</strong>tress, <strong>A</strong>lcohol, <strong>F</strong>atigue, <strong>E</strong>motion (sometimes Eating). Any yes is a reason to reconsider.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>DECIDE: responding to a change</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>D</strong>etect that a change has occurred. <strong>E</strong>stimate the need to react. <strong>C</strong>hoose a desirable outcome. <strong>I</strong>dentify actions to get there. <strong>D</strong>o the best action. <strong>E</strong>valuate the result.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>The five hazardous attitudes and their antidotes</h3>\n          <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n            <table>\n              <thead><tr><th>Attitude</th><th>Sounds like</th><th>Antidote</th></tr></thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr><td>Anti-authority</td><td>\"Don't tell me. Those rules are for other people.\"</td><td>Follow the rules. They are usually right.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Impulsivity</td><td>\"Do it quickly. Launch now, figure it out in the air.\"</td><td>Not so fast. Think first.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Invulnerability</td><td>\"It won't happen to me. I've flown here a hundred times.\"</td><td>It could happen to me.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Macho</td><td>\"I can do it. Watch this.\"</td><td>Taking chances is foolish.</td></tr>\n                <tr><td>Resignation</td><td>\"What's the use? It's out of my hands.\"</td><td>I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.</td></tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"callout callout-tip\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Test tip</span>\n            <p>Hazardous attitude questions give you a quote and ask which attitude it is, or give an attitude and ask for the antidote. Learn the five pairs as pairs.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h2 id=\"risk\">Risk management</h2>\n          <p>A <strong>hazard</strong> is a condition that could cause harm (a tree line, a crowd, gusty wind). <strong>Risk</strong> is the likelihood and severity of the harm that hazard could cause. The FAA's risk matrix scores likelihood (improbable to frequent) against severity (negligible to catastrophic). Risks in the red are mitigated until they are acceptable, or the flight does not happen. Mitigations include changing the launch site, adding a VO, waiting for wind to drop, reducing altitude, or saying no to the client. Most accidents trace to a chain of small decisions; break any link and the accident does not happen.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"emergencies\">Emergency procedures</h2>\n          <p>Under 107.21 the remote PIC may deviate from any rule to the extent necessary to meet an emergency, and must send a written report if the FAA asks. The best emergency procedure is the one you planned before takeoff:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Lost link.</strong> Know what your aircraft does when the control signal drops. Most execute a return-to-home at a preset altitude after a few seconds. Set that altitude above every obstacle between the aircraft and home, confirm the home point before every flight, and know how to cancel the return if the link comes back.</li>\n            <li><strong>Flyaway.</strong> Try to regain control (switch modes, re-establish the link, use the return function). If you are in controlled airspace, tell ATC immediately: what, where, altitude, direction. Note the last known position and battery remaining. Afterward, decide whether 107.9 requires an accident report.</li>\n            <li><strong>Low battery.</strong> Land as soon as practical. Warnings are based on capacity that is already draining faster than the estimate; a headwind on the return makes it worse. Never push a battery to finish a job.</li>\n            <li><strong>GPS loss.</strong> Most aircraft drop to an attitude mode: level, but drifting with the wind. Fly it manually and land. Practice attitude-mode flying somewhere open before you need it.</li>\n            <li><strong>Motor or propeller failure.</strong> A quadcopter cannot fly on three motors; aim for the safest impact point if any control remains. Hexacopters and octocopters may limp home. After any prop strike or bird strike, land as soon as safely possible and inspect before flying again.</li>\n            <li><strong>Loss of visual line of sight.</strong> Regain it immediately, with the VO's help. If you cannot, bring the aircraft back toward you or land it.</li>\n            <li><strong>Manned aircraft approaching.</strong> Yield: descend or move away, and never pass over, under, or ahead of it unless well clear. A low helicopter is the most common conflict for drone pilots.</li>\n            <li><strong>Fire.</strong> Lithium battery fires burn hot and reignite. Use a bucket of sand, a LiPo bag, or let it burn in a safe spot. Do not use water on a burning pack that is still charged.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"radio\">Radio communication</h2>\n          <p>You will rarely transmit on an aviation radio as a Part 107 pilot, but listening is one of the best situational awareness tools available, and the test expects you to understand what you hear.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>The phonetic alphabet:</strong> Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, Golf, Hotel, India, Juliett, Kilo, Lima, Mike, November, Oscar, Papa, Quebec, Romeo, Sierra, Tango, Uniform, Victor, Whiskey, X-ray, Yankee, Zulu. Numbers are spoken digit by digit; nine is \"niner\".</li>\n            <li><strong>CTAF</strong> is the common traffic advisory frequency at non-towered airports, usually a UNICOM frequency such as 122.8 or 122.95. Pilots announce their position and intentions: \"Millbrook traffic, Skyhawk 12345, turning base, runway 18, Millbrook.\" Hearing that tells you an aircraft is about to be low over the approach end of runway 18.</li>\n            <li><strong>ATIS</strong> is a recorded loop at towered airports with the weather, altimeter, runway in use, and notices, updated hourly and labeled with a phonetic letter. The wind on ATIS is magnetic.</li>\n            <li><strong>Tower, ground, and approach</strong> frequencies are in the airport data and the Chart Supplement. If ATC gives you an instruction while you are operating under an authorization, follow it.</li>\n            <li>A handheld aviation band receiver is legal to listen to without a license. Transmitting on aviation frequencies is for aircraft stations and should not be done casually from the ground.</li>\n            <li><strong>Standard phraseology</strong> keeps messages short and unambiguous: who you are calling, who you are, where you are, what you want.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"airports\">Airport operations</h2>\n          <p>107.43 prohibits interfering with operations and traffic patterns at any airport, heliport, or seaplane base, towered or not, authorized or not. To stay out of the way you need to know where manned aircraft will be.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Runway numbers</strong> are the magnetic heading divided by ten: runway 9 points east (090°), runway 27 is the same strip used westbound. Opposite ends differ by 18. Parallel runways add L, C, R.</li>\n            <li><strong>The traffic pattern</strong> is a rectangle flown at about 1,000 ft AGL: upwind (along the runway after takeoff), crosswind, downwind (parallel to the runway, opposite the landing direction), base (perpendicular, descending), and final (aligned with the runway, descending). Standard patterns use <strong>left turns</strong>; RP on the chart or Chart Supplement means right turns for that runway. Aircraft on base and final descend through your altitude.</li>\n            <li><strong>Wind indicators.</strong> A wind sock points downwind; aircraft take off and land into the wind. A tetrahedron points into the wind. The <strong>segmented circle</strong> around the indicator has L-shaped extensions that show the pattern direction for each runway.</li>\n            <li><strong>Airport beacons:</strong> white and green, civilian land airport; white and yellow, water airport; green, yellow, and white, heliport; two quick white flashes and green, military airport. A beacon on during the day usually means the weather is below VFR minimums.</li>\n            <li><strong>Runway markings</strong> are white; taxiway markings are yellow. A <strong>runway holding position</strong> marking is two solid and two dashed yellow lines; nobody crosses from the solid side without clearance. A displaced threshold (white arrows) is runway you may taxi on but not land on.</li>\n            <li><strong>Approach lighting and VASI/PAPI</strong> lights guide aircraft on final at a 3 degree glide path. Around any runway, the extended centerline, several miles out, is where aircraft are lowest.</li>\n            <li><strong>Helicopters</strong> do not follow fixed-wing patterns and often fly well below 400 ft, especially near hospitals, police facilities, and pipelines.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"night\">Night operations</h2>\n          <p>Allowed without a waiver since April 2021 if the remote PIC has current night training (the initial test or recurrent course taken since then) and the aircraft has anti-collision lighting visible for at least 3 statute miles with a flash rate sufficient to avoid a collision (107.29). The lights are also required during civil twilight: 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset in the contiguous United States. Beyond the rule, night flying is about your eyes:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>The retina has <strong>cones</strong> in the center (color, detail, need light) and <strong>rods</strong> around the edges (dim light, no color, poor detail). At night, <strong>off-center viewing</strong>, looking slightly to one side of an object, puts it on the rods.</li>\n            <li><strong>Dark adaptation</strong> takes about 30 minutes and is destroyed in seconds by a bright white light. Dim your screen, use red light at the control station, and avoid looking at headlights.</li>\n            <li><strong>Autokinesis:</strong> a single stationary light stared at in the dark appears to move. Keep your eyes scanning and use reference points. A bright anti-collision strobe also helps distinguish your aircraft from stars and towers.</li>\n            <li><strong>Depth and distance</strong> are hard to judge at night; obstacles without lights are invisible. Survey the site in daylight, note wires and towers, and set a conservative return-to-home altitude.</li>\n            <li>Fatigue and lower oxygen (even mild hypoxia, at altitude) degrade night vision first.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"physiology\">Physiological factors: drugs, alcohol, fatigue, stress</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> the legal limits (8 hours, 0.04 percent) are minimums. Alcohol impairs judgment and reaction time starting with the first drink, and hangover effects persist long after the blood alcohol is zero. 107.27 applies 91.17 to every crewmember.</li>\n            <li><strong>Drugs:</strong> many over-the-counter and prescription medications impair performance: antihistamines, sleep aids, cold medicines, muscle relaxants, some blood pressure drugs. 91.17 prohibits acting as a crewmember while using any drug that affects your faculties in a way contrary to safety. When in doubt, do not fly, and ask a doctor or an aviation medical examiner.</li>\n            <li><strong>Fatigue:</strong> acute fatigue comes from a long day or short sleep and is cured by rest. Chronic fatigue builds over weeks and needs a change in habits. Fatigue narrows attention and slows decisions much like alcohol does.</li>\n            <li><strong>Stress:</strong> a little sharpens focus, a lot narrows it to the wrong thing. Acute stress (an angry client, a close call) and chronic stress (money, family) both degrade judgment. It is the S in IMSAFE.</li>\n            <li><strong>Dehydration and heat:</strong> remote pilots stand outdoors for hours. Headache, fatigue, and poor concentration are early signs. Water, shade, and breaks are part of the plan.</li>\n            <li><strong>Hyperventilation and hypoxia</strong> show up on the test from the manned-aircraft syllabus: hyperventilation (rapid breathing from anxiety) causes dizziness and tingling, fixed by slowing the breathing; hypoxia (lack of oxygen) matters at high elevation sites.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"maintenance\">Maintenance and inspection</h2>\n          <p>Part 107 has no maintenance regulation beyond the requirement that the aircraft be in a condition for safe operation (107.15) and be inspected before flight (107.49). Advisory Circular 107-2 recommends:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Follow the manufacturer's maintenance schedule and procedures. If there is none, develop your own based on experience and operating conditions, covering inspection intervals, wear items (propellers, motors, batteries), and firmware.</li>\n            <li>Keep records: flights, battery cycles, repairs, firmware versions. Not required by Part 107, but it is how you notice trends, satisfy insurers and waivers, and prove due diligence.</li>\n            <li>Inspect after any hard landing, crash, or unusual vibration before flying again.</li>\n            <li>Install firmware updates at home and verify settings (return-to-home altitude, geofence, failsafes) with a test flight before the next job.</li>\n            <li>Battery care: store partially charged at the manufacturer's storage voltage, charge in a fire-safe container, never charge a hot, swollen, or damaged pack, and retire packs that have been in a crash.</li>\n            <li>Replace propellers at the first chip or crack. They are the cheapest part on the aircraft and the most common cause of vibration and crashes.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"tips\">Test tips for operations questions</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Memory aids are tested directly: know what PAVE, IMSAFE, DECIDE, and the five hazardous attitudes stand for.</li>\n            <li>Airport questions assume you understand the traffic pattern and runway numbering. Sketch the rectangle once and it stays with you.</li>\n            <li>The \"right\" answer to any emergency question is the conservative one: land, yield, regain line of sight, inspect.</li>\n            <li>Physiology questions come from the manned-pilot handbook, so expect vocabulary like hyperventilation and dark adaptation even though you are on the ground.</li>\n          </ul>"}],"references":[{"id":"regs","title":"Part 107 cheat sheet","short":"Regulations","intro":"The whole rule in plain English, in the order the Code of Federal Regulations prints it, plus the two other parts every remote pilot lives with: Part 89 (Remote ID) and Part 48 (registration). Section numbers are what the test and the FAA cite. The current legal text is always at ecfr.gov.","html":"<h2 id=\"sec0\">Subpart A: General</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.1</div><div><h4>Applicability</h4><p>Part 107 applies to civil small unmanned aircraft (under 55 lb) operated in the United States, except air carrier operations, aircraft flown under the recreational exception (49 U.S.C. 44809), amateur rockets, moored balloons, and public aircraft that choose other rules.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.3</div><div><h4>Definitions</h4><p><strong>Control station</strong>: the equipment used to control the aircraft. <strong>Small unmanned aircraft</strong>: weighs less than 55 lb on takeoff including everything on board or attached. <strong>Visual line of sight</strong>: the unaided ability to see the aircraft well enough to know where it is and what it is doing. <strong>Visual observer</strong>: a person designated to help the remote PIC see and avoid traffic and hazards.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.5</div><div><h4>Falsification, reproduction, or alteration</h4><p>No false statements on any application, report, or record. Grounds for denial, suspension, or revocation of any certificate.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.7</div><div><h4>Inspection, testing, and demonstration of compliance</h4><p>On request from the FAA, NTSB, TSA, or any federal, state, or local law enforcement officer, the remote PIC must present the remote pilot certificate, the aircraft registration, and any other required document, and must make the aircraft and records available for inspection or testing.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.9</div><div><h4>Accident reporting</h4><p>Report to the FAA within 10 calendar days any operation that causes serious injury or loss of consciousness to any person, or damage to property other than the aircraft of $500 or more (repair cost or fair market value, whichever is lower).</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec1\">Subpart B: Operating rules</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.11</div><div><h4>Applicability</h4><p>Subpart B applies to every small unmanned aircraft operation under Part 107.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.12</div><div><h4>Requirement for a remote pilot certificate</h4><p>The remote PIC must hold a remote pilot certificate with a small UAS rating and meet the recency requirement of 107.65. Anyone else manipulating the controls must be under the remote PIC's direct supervision.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.13</div><div><h4>Registration</h4><p>The aircraft must be registered as required by Part 47 or Part 48.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.15</div><div><h4>Condition for safe operation</h4><p>Only operate an aircraft that is in a condition for safe operation. Check before flight, and discontinue the flight if continuing would be hazardous.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.17</div><div><h4>Medical condition</h4><p>No participation by anyone who knows, or has reason to know, of a physical or mental condition that would interfere with safe operation.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.19</div><div><h4>Remote pilot in command</h4><p>A remote PIC must be designated before or during the flight. The remote PIC is directly responsible for and is the final authority as to the operation, must ensure the aircraft poses no undue hazard if control is lost, and must ensure compliance with all of Part 107.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.21</div><div><h4>In-flight emergency</h4><p>In an emergency the remote PIC may deviate from any rule of Part 107 to the extent needed to meet it. Send a written report if the FAA asks.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.23</div><div><h4>Hazardous operation</h4><p>No careless or reckless operation that endangers life or property. No dropping objects in a way that creates an undue hazard.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.25</div><div><h4>Operation from a moving vehicle or aircraft</h4><p>No operation from a moving aircraft. Operation from a moving land or water vehicle is allowed only over a sparsely populated area and not while carrying another person's property for compensation or hire.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.27</div><div><h4>Alcohol and drugs</h4><p>Sections 91.17 and 91.19 apply: no acting as a crewmember within 8 hours of drinking, while under the influence, with a blood alcohol concentration of 0.04 percent or more, or while using any drug that affects safety. No knowingly carrying illegal drugs.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.29</div><div><h4>Operation at night and in civil twilight</h4><p>At night the remote PIC must have completed the current initial test or recurrent training, and the aircraft must have anti-collision lighting visible for at least 3 statute miles with a flash rate sufficient to avoid a collision. The same lighting is required during civil twilight (30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset in the contiguous United States; Alaska uses the Air Almanac). Intensity may be reduced only in the interest of safety.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.31</div><div><h4>Visual line of sight</h4><p>The remote PIC, the person on the controls, or a VO must be able to see the aircraft throughout the flight with vision unaided by any device other than corrective lenses, well enough to know its location, attitude, altitude, and direction, to observe the airspace for traffic and hazards, and to ensure it does not endanger anyone. Vision aids such as binoculars may be used only momentarily.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.33</div><div><h4>Visual observer</h4><p>If a VO is used, the remote PIC, control operator, and VO must maintain effective communication at all times, and the VO must be able to see the aircraft unaided and keep the remote PIC informed of its position and of hazards.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.35</div><div><h4>Operation of multiple small unmanned aircraft</h4><p>One person may not act as remote PIC, control operator, or VO for more than one aircraft at a time.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.36</div><div><h4>Carriage of hazardous material</h4><p>Prohibited. Not waivable.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.37</div><div><h4>Operation near aircraft; right-of-way rules</h4><p>Yield the right of way to all aircraft, airborne vehicles, and launch and reentry vehicles. Yielding means giving way and not passing over, under, or ahead unless well clear. No operating so close to another aircraft as to create a collision hazard.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.39</div><div><h4>Operation over human beings</h4><p>No flight over a person unless that person is directly participating, is under a covered structure or inside a stationary vehicle that can protect them, or the operation meets Subpart D (Category 1, 2, 3, or 4).</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.41</div><div><h4>Operation in certain airspace</h4><p>Prior ATC authorization is required in Class B, C, or D airspace and within the lateral boundaries of a Class E surface area designated for an airport.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.43</div><div><h4>Operation in the vicinity of airports</h4><p>No interference with operations and traffic patterns at any airport, heliport, or seaplane base.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.45</div><div><h4>Operation in prohibited or restricted areas</h4><p>Permission from the using or controlling agency is required.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.47</div><div><h4>Flight restrictions in the proximity of certain areas designated by NOTAM</h4><p>Comply with the special security instructions of 99.7 and the TFRs of 91.137 through 91.145, including the standing stadium restriction.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.49</div><div><h4>Preflight familiarization, inspection, and actions for aircraft operation</h4><p>Before every flight: assess weather, airspace and flight restrictions, the location of people and property, and ground hazards; brief all participants on conditions, emergency and contingency procedures, roles, and hazards; ensure control links work, there is enough power, and any payload is secure and does not affect controllability.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.51</div><div><h4>Operating limitations</h4><p>Groundspeed no more than 87 knots (100 mph). Altitude no more than 400 ft AGL, unless within a 400 ft radius of a structure and no more than 400 ft above its top. Visibility at least 3 statute miles from the control station. Stay 500 ft below and 2,000 ft horizontally from clouds.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec2\">Subpart C: Remote pilot certification</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.53</div><div><h4>Applicability</h4><p>Subpart C sets the requirements for the remote pilot certificate with a small UAS rating.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.57</div><div><h4>Offenses involving alcohol or drugs</h4><p>A conviction for growing, processing, selling, possessing, or using drugs, or a conviction involving alcohol or drugs while operating an aircraft or motor vehicle, is grounds for denial of an application for up to one year and for suspension or revocation.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.59</div><div><h4>Refusal to submit to an alcohol test</h4><p>Refusing a test or refusing to release results is grounds for denial for up to one year and for suspension or revocation.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.61</div><div><h4>Eligibility</h4><p>At least 16 years old; able to read, speak, write, and understand English; no condition that would interfere with safe operation; and a passing knowledge test score within the previous 24 calendar months, or for current Part 61 pilots, completion of the FAA's online initial training.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.63</div><div><h4>Issuance of a remote pilot certificate</h4><p>Apply on FAA Form 8710-13 (through IACRA) with the knowledge test report or training certificate. TSA security vetting applies.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.64</div><div><h4>Temporary certificate</h4><p>A temporary certificate is valid for up to 120 days, or until the permanent certificate arrives, or until notice that the certificate is denied.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.65</div><div><h4>Aeronautical knowledge recency</h4><p>To act as remote PIC you must have, within the previous 24 calendar months, passed the initial knowledge test, completed the FAA recurrent training, or (for Part 61 pilots with a current flight review) completed the applicable online training.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.67</div><div><h4>Knowledge tests: general procedures and passing grades</h4><p>Bring acceptable identification with your name, photo, date of birth, and signature. The FAA sets the passing grade.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.69</div><div><h4>Knowledge tests: cheating or other unauthorized conduct</h4><p>Copying, helping, using unauthorized materials, or removing test material makes the applicant ineligible for any certificate or rating for one year and is grounds for suspension or revocation of any certificate held.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.71</div><div><h4>Retesting after failure</h4><p>Wait 14 calendar days after a failed test before retaking it.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.73</div><div><h4>Knowledge and training</h4><p>The initial test covers: regulations; airspace classification, operating requirements, and flight restrictions; aviation weather sources and effects of weather; loading and performance; emergency procedures; crew resource management; radio communication procedures; determining aircraft performance; physiological effects of drugs and alcohol; aeronautical decision-making and judgment; airport operations; maintenance and preflight inspection; and operation at night.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.74</div><div><h4>Recurrent training areas</h4><p>The recurrent course covers regulations, airspace and flight restrictions, emergency procedures, CRM, ADM, airport operations, maintenance and preflight inspection, and night operations.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.77</div><div><h4>Change of name or address</h4><p>Name changes go through the Airmen Certification Branch with documentation. After a change of permanent mailing address, the holder may not exercise privileges after 30 days unless the FAA has been notified.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.79</div><div><h4>Voluntary surrender of certificate</h4><p>A certificate may be surrendered for cancellation with a written request.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec3\">Subpart D: Operations over human beings</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.100 to 107.105</div><div><h4>Applicability and limitations</h4><p>Subpart D sets the aircraft and operating requirements that let a remote PIC fly over people without a waiver. The remote PIC must comply with all other operating rules and any operating limitations in the aircraft's declaration of compliance or airworthiness certificate.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.110</div><div><h4>Category 1</h4><p>Aircraft of 0.55 lb or less, including everything attached, with no exposed rotating parts that could lacerate skin, and no safety defects. Sustained flight over open-air assemblies requires Remote ID.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.115 to 107.120</div><div><h4>Category 2</h4><p>Aircraft with an FAA-accepted declaration of compliance showing it will not cause injury more severe than 11 ft-lb of kinetic energy on impact, with no exposed lacerating parts and no safety defects. Sustained flight over open-air assemblies requires Remote ID.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.125 to 107.130</div><div><h4>Category 3</h4><p>Same as Category 2 but at the 25 ft-lb level. No sustained flight over open-air assemblies. Over people only within a closed or restricted-access site where everyone has been notified, or with no sustained flight over any person not under cover.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.140</div><div><h4>Category 4</h4><p>Aircraft with an airworthiness certificate under Part 21, operated within its limitations and maintained under an approved program. Sustained flight over open-air assemblies requires Remote ID.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.145</div><div><h4>Operations over moving vehicles</h4><p>Categories 1, 2, and 3: only within a closed or restricted-access site where people are on notice, or with no sustained flight over the moving vehicle. Category 4: under the aircraft's operating limitations.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.150 to 107.195</div><div><h4>Declarations of compliance and means of compliance</h4><p>How manufacturers establish that an aircraft meets Category 2 or 3: an FAA-accepted means of compliance, a declaration of compliance, labeling, and record keeping.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec4\">Subpart E: Waivers</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.200</div><div><h4>Waiver policy and requirements</h4><p>The FAA may issue a certificate of waiver if the applicant shows the operation can be safely conducted under its terms. Apply through FAADroneZone. The remote PIC must comply with every condition in the waiver.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">107.205</div><div><h4>List of regulations subject to waiver</h4><p>107.25 (moving vehicle), 107.29(a)(2) and (b) (night, in part), 107.31 (VLOS), 107.33 (VO), 107.35 (multiple aircraft), 107.37(a) (yielding right of way), 107.39 (over people), 107.41 (certain airspace), 107.51 (operating limitations), and 107.145 (over moving vehicles). Everything else, including 107.27 alcohol and 107.36 hazmat, cannot be waived.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec5\">Part 89: Remote identification</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">89.105 to 89.120</div><div><h4>Operating requirements</h4><p>Since September 16, 2023, every unmanned aircraft that requires registration must broadcast Remote ID: either <strong>standard Remote ID</strong> built into the aircraft (aircraft and control station location, altitude, velocity, identity, time, emergency status) or a <strong>broadcast module</strong> listed on the registration (broadcasts the takeoff location; the aircraft must stay within VLOS of the operator). Remote ID must be working from takeoff to shutdown and may not be disabled. The FAA's enforcement discretion period ended March 16, 2024.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">Subpart C</div><div><h4>FAA-Recognized Identification Areas (FRIAs)</h4><p>The one exception to broadcasting: aircraft without Remote ID may be flown within VLOS inside a FRIA. FRIAs are requested by community-based organizations and educational institutions and are shown on the FAA's UAS data map.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec6\">Part 48: Registration</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">48.15, 48.30</div><div><h4>Who must register, and the fee</h4><p>Aircraft over 0.55 lb must be registered; under Part 107 every aircraft must be registered regardless of weight, because the small-aircraft exemption applies only to the recreational exception. $5 per aircraft, valid 3 years, through FAADroneZone.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">48.100 to 48.115</div><div><h4>Registration details</h4><p>Part 107 aircraft are registered individually (recreational flyers register once for all their aircraft). The registrant must be 13 or older and a U.S. citizen or permanent resident, or a foreign operator may receive a recognition of ownership.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">48.205</div><div><h4>Marking</h4><p>Display the registration number legibly on an external surface of the aircraft, by any method that stays affixed for the life of the aircraft.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2 id=\"sec7\">Rules borrowed from elsewhere</h2>\n      <div class=\"reg-list\">\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">91.17, 91.19</div><div><h4>Alcohol and drugs</h4><p>The 8 hour, 0.04 percent, under-the-influence, and impairing-drug rules, applied to Part 107 crews by 107.27.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">91.137 to 91.145, 99.7</div><div><h4>Temporary flight restrictions and special security instructions</h4><p>Disaster and hazard areas, presidential and VIP movements, space operations, major sporting events, and national security restrictions. Applied to Part 107 by 107.47. Published by NOTAM.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">49 U.S.C. 44809</div><div><h4>Exception for limited recreational operations</h4><p>Recreational flyers follow this statute instead of Part 107: fly for fun only, within a community-based organization's guidelines, pass the free TRUST test, stay in Class G or get authorization, 400 ft, VLOS, and register if over 0.55 lb. The moment a flight has a business purpose, Part 107 applies.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"reg\"><div class=\"cite\">NPS Policy Memo 14-05</div><div><h4>National parks</h4><p>The National Park Service prohibits launching, landing, or operating unmanned aircraft within park boundaries without a special use permit. Other federal land managers (Forest Service, BLM, Fish and Wildlife) have their own rules, as do states, counties, and cities for their property.</p></div></div>\n      </div>","sections":[{"id":"sec0","title":"Subpart A: General"},{"id":"sec1","title":"Subpart B: Operating rules"},{"id":"sec2","title":"Subpart C: Remote pilot certification"},{"id":"sec3","title":"Subpart D: Operations over human beings"},{"id":"sec4","title":"Subpart E: Waivers"},{"id":"sec5","title":"Part 89: Remote identification"},{"id":"sec6","title":"Part 48: Registration"},{"id":"sec7","title":"Rules borrowed from elsewhere"}]},{"id":"safety","title":"Safety","short":"Safety","intro":"Part 107 exists because a two-pound aircraft falling 400 feet can hurt someone and a lost drone can meet a helicopter. The test checks that you know the rules; this page is about the habits that keep you out of the accident reports. Print the checklist. Use it every flight.","sections":[{"id":"gonogo","title":"The go/no-go decision"},{"id":"checklist","title":"Preflight checklist"},{"id":"airspace","title":"Airspace and restrictions"},{"id":"weather","title":"Weather limits"},{"id":"people","title":"People, roads, and property"},{"id":"emergency","title":"Emergency plan"},{"id":"battery","title":"Battery safety"},{"id":"night","title":"Night operations"},{"id":"law","title":"Privacy, local law, insurance"},{"id":"reporting","title":"Reporting"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"gonogo\">The go/no-go decision</h2>\n          <p>Every accident chain has a point where someone could have stopped it. Decide in advance what would stop you, so the decision is made before the client is standing next to you. The FAA's <strong>PAVE</strong> framework is the simplest version:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Pilot:</strong> rested, sober, healthy, current, and competent with this aircraft in these conditions? Run IMSAFE: illness, medication, stress, alcohol, fatigue, emotion.</li>\n            <li><strong>Aircraft:</strong> inspected, charged, props sound, firmware stable, failsafes set, within weight limits with today's payload?</li>\n            <li><strong>Environment:</strong> airspace, TFRs, weather, wind at altitude, sun angle, obstacles, wires, birds, people, traffic?</li>\n            <li><strong>External pressures:</strong> what is pushing you to fly when you should not? A deadline, a fee, a crowd watching, \"we drove all this way.\" Name the pressure out loud and it loses most of its power.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"callout callout-safety\">\n            <span class=\"callout-title\">Personal minimums</span>\n            <p>Write down limits stricter than the law: maximum wind, minimum visibility, minimum battery for return, maximum distance, no flights over roads with traffic, no flights when the hazardous-attitude voice starts talking. Then follow them on the day you least want to.</p>\n          </div>\n\n          <h2 id=\"checklist\">Preflight checklist</h2>\n          <p>Built around the requirements of 107.49. Print this page; the checklist prints cleanly.</p>\n          <h3>The night before</h3>\n          <ul class=\"checklist\">\n            <li>Airspace checked for the site: class, authorization obtained if needed (LAANC or FAADroneZone), facility map ceiling noted</li>\n            <li>TFRs and NOTAMs checked (tfr.faa.gov, NOTAM search, or an FAA-approved app)</li>\n            <li>Local rules checked: launch site permission, park rules, city ordinances</li>\n            <li>Forecast checked: TAF, wind at altitude, precipitation, temperature, sunrise and sunset times</li>\n            <li>Batteries fully charged and inspected (no swelling, dents, or heat); controller and tablet charged</li>\n            <li>Firmware current and verified with a prior test flight; no updates on the day</li>\n            <li>Memory cards empty and formatted; spare props packed</li>\n            <li>Remote pilot certificate, registration, and recurrent training certificate in the bag</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>At the site, before powering on</h3>\n          <ul class=\"checklist\">\n            <li>Walk the site: wires, towers, trees, birds, people, vehicles, animals, water</li>\n            <li>Confirm the launch and recovery area is clear and will stay clear; set cones or a perimeter</li>\n            <li>Check actual weather: visibility at least 3 sm, clouds give 500 ft clearance, wind within your minimums</li>\n            <li>Crew briefing: roles, VO positions, communication words, hazards, emergency and lost-link plan, stop word</li>\n            <li>Airframe: arms, props (tight, no chips), motors spin freely, gimbal clamp removed, landing gear, lights</li>\n            <li>Payload secure; takeoff weight within limits</li>\n            <li>Battery seated and locked; charge level confirmed</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>After powering on</h3>\n          <ul class=\"checklist\">\n            <li>Controller linked; telemetry, video, and control inputs verified</li>\n            <li>GPS lock and home point confirmed; compass calibrated if the app asks</li>\n            <li>Return-to-home altitude set above every obstacle; lost-link behavior confirmed</li>\n            <li>Remote ID broadcasting (standard or module)</li>\n            <li>Anti-collision light on if flying at night or in civil twilight</li>\n            <li>Short hover check: stable, responsive, no vibration or unusual sounds; land and inspect if anything is off</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>After the flight</h3>\n          <ul class=\"checklist\">\n            <li>Motors off, battery removed, props inspected</li>\n            <li>Log the flight: time, battery used, location, anything unusual</li>\n            <li>Debrief the crew: what surprised you, what to change next time</li>\n            <li>Batteries cooled before charging; stored at storage charge if not flying tomorrow</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"airspace\">Airspace and restrictions</h2>\n          <p>Controlled airspace near an airport requires authorization (Class B, C, D, and Class E surface areas). LAANC gives it in minutes at or below the facility map ceiling; a zero grid or an altitude above the ceiling needs a FAADroneZone request, which can take weeks. Authorizations are for a specific polygon, altitude, and time; flying outside any of them is unauthorized.</p>\n          <p>TFRs are the trap that catches careful pilots: they appear with hours of notice for wildfires, VIP visits, disasters, and events. The stadium restriction is standing: 3 nm and 3,000 ft around major sporting events from an hour before to an hour after. Check every single flight, not every single day.</p>\n          <p>Even in Class G, 107.43 prohibits interfering with airport traffic. Near any airport, know the runway orientation and stay away from the extended centerlines, where aircraft are lowest. Helicopters, crop dusters, and medical flights operate below 400 ft far from any airport; a visual observer earns their keep here.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"weather\">Weather limits</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Visibility:</strong> 3 statute miles from the control station is the legal floor. Haze, smoke, and low sun can take you below it without a cloud in the sky.</li>\n            <li><strong>Clouds:</strong> 500 ft below and 2,000 ft horizontal. A ceiling under 900 ft AGL means you cannot legally fly at 400 ft.</li>\n            <li><strong>Wind:</strong> the manufacturer's limit is for a light aircraft in steady wind. Gusts, payload, and cold batteries all cut into it. Wind is stronger at 400 ft than at your face, and the return leg is upwind for half of all flights.</li>\n            <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> cold reduces battery capacity and can trigger sudden power loss; heat shortens battery life and overheats electronics. Warm packs before cold-weather flights and shade them in summer.</li>\n            <li><strong>Precipitation:</strong> most small aircraft are not rated for rain. Water in motors and connectors fails later, not now.</li>\n            <li><strong>Thunderstorms:</strong> the gust front arrives before the rain. Any storm within sight is a no-go.</li>\n            <li><strong>Density altitude:</strong> hot, high sites reduce thrust and endurance. Plan lighter and shorter.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"people\">People, roads, and property</h2>\n          <p>Flight over people is prohibited unless they are participating, protected by a structure or stationary vehicle, or the aircraft meets a Category 1 to 4 standard. Treat the categories as what they are: a declaration that the aircraft is unlikely to cause serious injury, not a promise that it will not fall. Bystanders, by definition, did not consent to the risk.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Establish a takeoff and landing perimeter and keep it. Most crashes happen within a few feet of the ground at launch and recovery.</li>\n            <li>Plan flight paths that avoid sustained flight over roads with moving traffic, crowds, and neighboring property. Go around, not over.</li>\n            <li>A visual observer and a \"people\" crew member who manages bystanders are two different jobs.</li>\n            <li>Over a closed set or job site, make sure everyone present has been briefed; that is what \"on notice\" means in Category 3.</li>\n            <li>Dropping or delivering an object is allowed only if it creates no undue hazard. Think about what happens if the release fails halfway.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"emergency\">Emergency plan</h2>\n          <p>Write it in the briefing so nobody has to invent it under stress:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Lost link:</strong> the aircraft will return to the home point at the set altitude. The remote PIC calls \"lost link,\" the VO keeps eyes on the aircraft, everyone clears the landing area.</li>\n            <li><strong>Flyaway:</strong> remote PIC attempts recovery (mode switch, return function, re-link). If in controlled airspace, call the tower or approach control with position, altitude, and direction. Note the last known position and battery remaining. Report under 107.9 if the aircraft causes a qualifying injury or damage.</li>\n            <li><strong>Manned aircraft:</strong> descend and move away; never cross its path. Land if in doubt.</li>\n            <li><strong>Low battery or power anomaly:</strong> land at the nearest safe spot, which is not necessarily the home point.</li>\n            <li><strong>Loss of visual line of sight:</strong> VO calls it, remote PIC brings the aircraft back toward the crew, climbs only if it is safe to do so.</li>\n            <li><strong>Injury:</strong> secure the aircraft first (a spinning prop is still dangerous), then first aid and emergency services.</li>\n            <li><strong>Battery fire:</strong> move it away from people and fuel, use sand or a LiPo bag, do not use water on a charged pack, and let it burn out if nothing else is safe.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"battery\">Battery safety</h2>\n          <p>Lithium batteries cause more drone-related injuries on the ground than crashes do in the air.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Charge in a fire-resistant bag or metal box, on a non-flammable surface, never unattended, never overnight.</li>\n            <li>Never charge a pack that is hot, swollen, dented, punctured, or has been in a crash. Retire it.</li>\n            <li>Store packs at the manufacturer's storage charge (around 3.8 volts per cell, often shown as 50 to 60 percent), not full, in a cool dry place.</li>\n            <li>Let packs cool after flight before charging, and warm them before cold flights.</li>\n            <li>Dispose of retired packs at a battery recycling point after discharging them safely; never in household trash.</li>\n            <li><strong>Air travel:</strong> spare lithium batteries go in carry-on only, terminals protected. Packs under 100 Wh need no airline approval; 100 to 160 Wh require airline approval and are usually limited to two spares. Check the airline before you pack.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"night\">Night operations</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Legal requirements: current training, and an anti-collision light visible 3 statute miles with a flash rate sufficient to avoid a collision, on at night and in civil twilight.</li>\n            <li>Survey the site in daylight. Wires, guy lines, and tree limbs are invisible at night.</li>\n            <li>Give your eyes 30 minutes to adapt, keep the control station lighting dim or red, and use off-center viewing to track the aircraft.</li>\n            <li>Beware autokinesis (a stationary light appears to move) and false perception of distance. The strobe is your friend.</li>\n            <li>A visual observer is close to essential at night, and position lights plus the strobe help the crew tell orientation.</li>\n            <li>Set a higher return-to-home altitude than you would in daylight, and a larger battery reserve.</li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2 id=\"law\">Privacy, local law, and insurance</h2>\n          <p>The FAA regulates the air; nearly everything else is state and local. Trespass, nuisance, voyeurism, and recording laws apply to drone operators the way they apply to anyone with a camera. Cities and counties can regulate takeoff and landing on their property, and the National Park Service bans drones in parks. Some states have specific drone statutes covering critical infrastructure, prisons, and privacy.</p>\n          <p>The FAA does not require liability insurance for Part 107 operations, but clients, venues, and property owners usually do, and a claim after an accident can exceed anything a small business can absorb. Policies are inexpensive relative to the risk. Keep flight and maintenance logs: they are the evidence of a careful operator.</p>\n\n          <h2 id=\"reporting\">Reporting</h2>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Accidents (107.9):</strong> within 10 calendar days, report to the FAA through FAADroneZone any serious injury or loss of consciousness, or damage to property other than the aircraft of $500 or more.</li>\n            <li><strong>NTSB (49 CFR 830):</strong> the NTSB has its own notification rules for unmanned aircraft accidents involving serious injury or substantial damage to an aircraft over 300 lb; for most small drones the 107.9 FAA report is the one that applies.</li>\n            <li><strong>Emergency deviations (107.21):</strong> a written report only if the FAA asks for one.</li>\n            <li><strong>Safety reports (NASA ASRS):</strong> the Aviation Safety Reporting System accepts confidential reports from unmanned aircraft operators about near misses and mistakes. The data improves the system, and a timely report can carry limited protection from enforcement for inadvertent violations.</li>\n          </ul>"},{"id":"resources","title":"Resources and links","short":"Resources","intro":"Everything on this site is drawn from the FAA documents below, which are free. If this site and an FAA document ever disagree, the FAA document wins. Links were checked in October 2026; the FAA reorganizes its site occasionally, so search the title if a link breaks.","html":"<div class=\"grid grid-2\">\n        <div>\n          <h2>Getting certified</h2>\n          <ul class=\"links\">\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/commercial_operators/become_a_drone_pilot\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA: Become a Certificated Remote Pilot</a><span>The official step list, eligibility, and links to the PSI scheduling portal and study materials.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://iacra.faa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">IACRA</a><span>Create your FAA Tracking Number here, then apply for the certificate (Form 8710-13) after you pass.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://faa.psiexams.com\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">PSI: FAA knowledge test scheduling</a><span>Schedule and pay for the UAG exam ($175) at an FAA-approved test center.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs/uas_acs.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Remote Pilot Airman Certification Standards (FAA-S-ACS-10)</a><span>The exact list of what the test can ask, by knowledge area. Read it once so nothing surprises you.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/test_questions\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA sample knowledge test questions</a><span>Free official samples in the real format, including the Unmanned Aircraft General set.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/supplements\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Airman Knowledge Testing Supplements</a><span>The booklet of figures (chart excerpts, load factor chart, weather reports) you will be given at the test. Practice with the Sport, Recreational, Remote, and Private Pilot supplement.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/phak\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge</a><span>Free FAA textbook. The weather, aerodynamics, airspace, and ADM chapters are the source for most theory questions.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_107-2A.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Advisory Circular 107-2A</a><span>The FAA's guidance on how to comply with Part 107: preflight, maintenance, risk assessment, and more.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faasafety.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAASafety.gov</a><span>Recurrent training every 24 months (ALC-677, or ALC-515 for Part 61 pilots) and the initial course for Part 61 pilots (ALC-451). Free.</span></li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2>The rules themselves</h2>\n          <ul class=\"links\">\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-107\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">14 CFR Part 107 (eCFR)</a><span>The current legal text, always up to date.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-89\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">14 CFR Part 89, Remote Identification</a><span>Standard Remote ID, broadcast modules, and FRIAs.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-48\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">14 CFR Part 48, Registration</a><span>Who registers, fees, and marking.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/commercial_operators/operations_over_people\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA: Operations over people</a><span>The four categories explained, with the list of aircraft that have accepted declarations of compliance.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/commercial_operators/part_107_waivers\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA: Part 107 waivers</a><span>What can be waived, how to apply, and examples of granted waivers.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/getting_started/remote_id\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA: Remote ID</a><span>Compliance options and the list of approved aircraft and modules.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/articles/unmanned-aircraft-in-the-national-parks.htm\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">National Park Service: unmanned aircraft</a><span>Why you cannot launch or land in a national park.</span></li>\n          </ul>\n        </div>\n\n        <div>\n          <h2>Before every flight</h2>\n          <ul class=\"links\">\n            <li><a href=\"https://faadronezone-access.faa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAADroneZone</a><span>Register aircraft, apply for waivers and airspace authorizations, and file accident reports.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/getting_started/laanc\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA: LAANC</a><span>How automated airspace authorization works and the current list of approved service suppliers.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/commercial_operators/uas_facility_maps\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">UAS Facility Maps</a><span>The grid ceilings that LAANC can auto-approve around each airport.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/getting_started/b4ufly\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">B4UFLY</a><span>The FAA's airspace awareness service, delivered through approved apps.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://tfr.faa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Temporary flight restrictions</a><span>Every active TFR on a map. Check it every flight.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://notams.aim.faa.gov/notamSearch\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">NOTAM search</a><span>Notices to Air Missions by location or airport.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://aviationweather.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Aviation Weather Center</a><span>METARs, TAFs, graphical forecasts, SIGMETs, and PIREPs. Use it to practice decoding.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.1800wxbrief.com\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Flight Service (1800wxbrief)</a><span>Free standard briefings online or by phone at 1-800-WX-BRIEF.</span></li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2>Charts</h2>\n          <ul class=\"links\">\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/flight_info/aeronav/digital_products/vfr\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAA VFR charts (digital)</a><span>Free current sectionals. Download the one for your area and read it against the airspace module.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/flight_info/aeronav/digital_products/aero_guide\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Aeronautical Chart User's Guide</a><span>The legend for every symbol on a sectional. The source for the chart-reading section of this site.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/flight_info/aeronav/digital_products/dafd\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Chart Supplement</a><span>Airport details, tower hours, frequencies, and special use airspace contacts.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://skyvector.com\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">SkyVector</a><span>Free online sectional charts you can pan and zoom, with airport information. Excellent for practicing chart reading.</span></li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <h2>Apps and extras</h2>\n          <ul class=\"links\">\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.aloft.ai\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Aloft</a><span>FAA-approved LAANC provider; also delivers B4UFLY.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://airspacelink.com\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Airspace Link</a><span>FAA-approved LAANC provider with airspace and local-rule layers.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.uavforecast.com\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">UAV Forecast</a><span>Wind, gusts, cloud base, and GPS satellite forecasts aimed at drone pilots.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://www.windy.com\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Windy</a><span>Wind at multiple altitudes, useful for seeing how much stronger it is at 400 ft.</span></li>\n            <li><a href=\"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">NASA Aviation Safety Reporting System</a><span>Confidential safety reports, including from unmanned aircraft operators.</span></li>\n          </ul>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"panel\">\n        <h3>Other free study material</h3>\n        <p>Several drone training companies publish free Part 107 study guides, practice questions, and video courses, and many pilots pass with nothing more than those and the FAA documents above. This site is independent and recommends none of them in particular; search for a current free course, check its date, and prefer the ones that cite the FAA sources. Paid courses ($100 to $300) buy structure and a pass guarantee, not secret knowledge.</p>\n        <p>The only required cost is the $175 test fee. Anyone charging to \"renew\" or \"register\" your certificate is selling something the FAA provides free.</p>\n      </div>","sections":[]}],"steps":[{"id":"eligibility","num":1,"title":"Confirm you are eligible","meta":"","html":"<p>Under 14 CFR 107.61 you must be at least 16 years old, able to read, speak, write, and understand English, and in a physical and mental condition that lets you operate a drone safely. There is no medical certificate and no citizenship requirement, although the TSA vetting in step 7 applies to everyone.</p>\n            <p>Already hold a Part 61 pilot certificate with a current flight review? You skip the knowledge test: complete the FAA's free online initial training (ALC-451 on FAASafety.gov) and go straight to step 6.</p>"},{"id":"ftn","num":2,"title":"Get an FAA Tracking Number (FTN)","meta":"Cost: free. Time: 10 minutes.","html":"<p>Create an account at <a href=\"https://iacra.faa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">iacra.faa.gov</a>, the FAA's Integrated Airman Certification and Rating Application. Register as an applicant and the system assigns you an FTN. You will need it to schedule the test, and the test center will need it to report your score.</p>"},{"id":"study","num":3,"title":"Study","meta":"Cost: free. Time: most people need 15 to 25 hours of study spread over a few weeks.","html":"<p>This is the step that decides whether you pay $175 once or twice. Work through the <a href=\"#/study\">five study modules</a>, which follow the FAA's own list of knowledge areas in 107.73, then read the <a href=\"#/ref/regs\">regulations cheat sheet</a>. The FAA's free <em>Remote Pilot Small UAS Study Guide</em> and the <em>Airman Certification Standards</em> (both linked on the <a href=\"#/ref/resources\">resources page</a>) are the official sources.</p>\n            <p>Take <a href=\"#/exam\">practice exams</a> until you pass comfortably. Aim for 85 percent or better on full timed exams before you book, because the real pool is larger than any practice bank.</p>"},{"id":"schedule","num":4,"title":"Schedule the test with PSI","meta":"Cost: $175, paid when you schedule. Rescheduling inside 24 hours usually carries a fee.","html":"<p>The FAA contracts with PSI Services to deliver all airman knowledge tests. Create an account on PSI's FAA portal (linked from the FAA's <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/uas/commercial_operators/become_a_drone_pilot\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">Become a Certificated Remote Pilot</a> page), enter your FTN, and pick the exam called <strong>Unmanned Aircraft General, Small (UAG)</strong>. Choose a test center and time slot. Centers are often at flight schools, community colleges, and commercial testing offices.</p>"},{"id":"test","num":5,"title":"Take the test","meta":"Time: 2 hours maximum. Results: immediate.","html":"<p>Bring a government-issued photo ID that matches your IACRA name exactly. Arrive early. Personal items, phones, and notes stay in a locker. The center provides scratch paper, and the test software includes the <em>Airman Knowledge Testing Supplement</em>, the booklet of charts and figures that many questions refer to. Learn to read it before test day: you can download the supplement free from the FAA.</p>\n            <p>The test is 60 multiple-choice questions with three options each. You have 2 hours, which is more than enough; most people finish in under an hour. Mark questions you are unsure of and come back to them. A passing score is 70 percent, so you may miss 18 questions.</p>\n            <p>When you finish, the proctor prints your <strong>Airman Knowledge Test Report (AKTR)</strong> with your score and the codes of any knowledge areas you missed. Keep it. If you fail, you may retest after 14 calendar days.</p>"},{"id":"iacra","num":6,"title":"Apply for the certificate in IACRA","meta":"Cost: free. Time: 15 minutes. Test scores are valid for 24 calendar months, so do not sit on this.","html":"<p>Log back in to IACRA and start a new application for a <strong>Remote Pilot Certificate</strong> (FAA Form 8710-13). Enter the 17-digit knowledge test exam ID from your AKTR; IACRA matches it to your passing score automatically. Review, sign electronically, and submit. No examiner or instructor signature is needed.</p>"},{"id":"tsa","num":7,"title":"TSA security vetting and your temporary certificate","meta":"Time: typically a few days to a few weeks.","html":"<p>Every applicant is vetted by the Transportation Security Administration. Nothing is required of you. When it clears, IACRA emails you a link to print a <strong>temporary remote pilot certificate</strong>, valid for up to 120 days. You may fly under Part 107 as soon as you have it.</p>"},{"id":"card","num":8,"title":"Permanent card arrives by mail","meta":"Time: usually several weeks after the temporary certificate.","html":"<p>Your plastic certificate is mailed to the address in IACRA. It has no expiration date. Carry it, or the temporary certificate, whenever you fly, along with your aircraft registration: 107.7 requires you to present them on request to the FAA, NTSB, TSA, or law enforcement. Report any change of address within 30 days (107.77).</p>"},{"id":"register","num":9,"title":"Register your aircraft and meet Remote ID","meta":"Cost: $5 per aircraft. A Remote ID broadcast module, if your aircraft lacks built-in Remote ID, is a one-time purchase.","html":"<p>Every aircraft flown under Part 107 must be registered at <a href=\"https://faadronezone-access.faa.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAADroneZone</a> regardless of weight: $5 per aircraft, valid 3 years, number marked on the outside of the aircraft. The aircraft must also broadcast Remote ID (standard Remote ID or an add-on broadcast module listed on the registration), unless you only fly it inside an FAA-Recognized Identification Area.</p>"},{"id":"recurrent","num":10,"title":"Stay current: free training every 24 months","meta":"Cost: free. Anyone charging you to \"renew\" your Part 107 is selling something you can do yourself in an afternoon.","html":"<p>To keep exercising your privileges you must complete the FAA's free online recurrent training within every 24 calendar months: course <strong>ALC-677, Part 107 Small UAS Recurrent</strong> on <a href=\"https://www.faasafety.gov\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"ext\">FAASafety.gov</a> (ALC-515 if you are a current Part 61 pilot). It takes about two hours and ends with an open-book quiz. Print the completion certificate and keep it with your card. If you let it lapse, you keep the certificate but may not act as remote PIC until you finish the training.</p>"}],"checklist":["Government photo ID, name matching IACRA exactly","Your FTN (write it down; the center will ask)","PSI confirmation email or number","Know the supplement figures: sectional excerpts, load factor chart, METAR and TAF examples","Sleep. The regulations questions are pure recall and tired brains swap numbers.","Arrive 30 minutes early; late arrivals may forfeit the fee"],"faq":"<h4>Do I need a license to fly for fun?</h4>\n          <p>No. Recreational flyers take the free TRUST test instead. Part 107 is required the moment a flight is for business, compensation, or any non-recreational purpose, including a free favor for a friend's business.</p>\n          <h4>Is there a drone size limit?</h4>\n          <p>Part 107 covers aircraft under 55 lb at takeoff. Heavier aircraft need a different approval.</p>\n          <h4>How hard is it?</h4>\n          <p>Pass rates for the initial UAG test have run in the low-to-mid 80 percent range in recent FAA reporting. Nearly everyone who fails underestimated charts, airspace, and weather.</p>\n          <h4>Can I fly for money before the card arrives?</h4>\n          <p>Yes, as soon as you have the temporary certificate from IACRA.</p>"};
